1
00:00:01,650 --> 00:00:10,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this Sutta I'm bringing up, because I'm interested in the topic of cessation of contact.

2
00:00:15,224 --> 00:00:22,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's Anguttara 6.63, called the Penetrative Discourse.

3
00:00:29,707 --> 00:00:34,129
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, sensual pleasures should be understood.

4
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:39,232
[SPEAKER_00]: The feelings should be understood.

5
00:00:39,292 --> 00:00:40,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceptions should be understood.

6
00:00:41,093 --> 00:00:42,514
[SPEAKER_00]: The taints should be understood.

7
00:00:42,654 --> 00:00:43,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Karma should be understood.

8
00:00:44,454 --> 00:00:45,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering should be understood.

9
00:00:46,896 --> 00:00:50,097
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's these six topics that need to be understood.

10
00:00:50,698 --> 00:00:51,138
[SPEAKER_00]: But also,

11
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:56,888
[SPEAKER_00]: The source and origin of sensual pleasures and so on should be understood.

12
00:00:57,088 --> 00:01:00,154
[SPEAKER_00]: The diversity of sensual pleasures and so on should be understood.

13
00:01:01,353 --> 00:01:03,414
[SPEAKER_00]: The result should be understood.

14
00:01:03,734 --> 00:01:05,654
[SPEAKER_00]: The cessation should be understood.

15
00:01:06,034 --> 00:01:08,355
[SPEAKER_00]: And the way leading to the cessation should be understood.

16
00:01:09,055 --> 00:01:09,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Of those six things.

17
00:01:10,076 --> 00:01:11,156
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to all those things, yeah.

18
00:01:11,696 --> 00:01:16,437
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first and foremost, should be understood.

19
00:01:18,098 --> 00:01:18,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

20
00:01:18,778 --> 00:01:19,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

21
00:01:20,138 --> 00:01:22,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So... A person should ask himself, what is understanding?

22
00:01:22,919 --> 00:01:23,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

23
00:01:23,399 --> 00:01:24,540
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of understanding?

24
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:25,820
[SPEAKER_00]: But those, those... Yeah.

25
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:28,381
[SPEAKER_00]: But through that kind of questioning of...

26
00:01:29,492 --> 00:01:31,352
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the source and origin of sensual pleasures?

27
00:01:31,912 --> 00:01:33,713
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the diversity in sensual pleasures?

28
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:34,573
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the result?

29
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:36,213
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cessation?

30
00:01:36,753 --> 00:01:38,214
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the way leading to the cessation?

31
00:01:38,234 --> 00:01:41,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can be thinking about these things, questioning this.

32
00:01:41,754 --> 00:01:46,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I'm just trying to highlight that it should be understood, must be understood.

33
00:01:46,755 --> 00:01:52,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, I had this experience of freedom from sensuality and I haven't understood it.

34
00:01:52,276 --> 00:01:53,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why does it need to be understood?

35
00:01:53,936 --> 00:01:55,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only way to free yourself from it.

36
00:01:56,357 --> 00:01:58,417
[SPEAKER_00]: The lack of understanding is the root of everything.

37
00:01:59,832 --> 00:02:14,680
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like, even when people practice the dhamma, they practice with this notion of you're going to have a special experience as a result of whatever methods and efforts you're making, then it's going to have these revelations and

38
00:02:15,516 --> 00:02:24,004
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not have any revelations that matter, unless you make the effort to understand, and then your revelations will be, ah, now I understood it.

39
00:02:24,385 --> 00:02:25,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, okay, that's what's enshrined.

40
00:02:26,247 --> 00:02:29,690
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why... what's the other one?

41
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:30,631
[SPEAKER_01]: The feeling.

42
00:02:30,671 --> 00:02:31,512
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what feeling is.

43
00:02:31,972 --> 00:02:33,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's the nature of the feeling.

44
00:02:34,054 --> 00:02:36,196
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nature of things to be perceived.

45
00:02:36,336 --> 00:02:37,417
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to understand it.

46
00:02:38,142 --> 00:02:43,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like just do random stuff and then these these results will magically occur to you.

47
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:44,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you... that's what I mean.

48
00:02:44,711 --> 00:02:46,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you ask yourself, what is understanding?

49
00:02:46,634 --> 00:02:48,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how do I understand anything?

50
00:02:49,718 --> 00:02:53,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I follow a set of sort of prescribed motions?

51
00:02:54,652 --> 00:02:58,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And arrive at understanding, if I do step one, step two, step three... No.

52
00:02:59,595 --> 00:03:00,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And everybody knows that.

53
00:03:00,636 --> 00:03:13,485
[SPEAKER_01]: You just recall back in the... when you were in the school, when they... when they sort of, kind of, parrot you things out that you must memorize, and you memorize it, and you repeat, and you get a top mark, and you haven't understood a clue.

54
00:03:15,527 --> 00:03:20,109
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't actually made that internal connection to it, that you recognize the nature of it.

55
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:23,991
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, give it some time, and you don't remember a single thing.

56
00:03:24,431 --> 00:03:25,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Even while you're still in school, by the way.

57
00:03:26,272 --> 00:03:30,854
[SPEAKER_01]: But things you understood, you still remember, 30, 40 years later.

58
00:03:30,894 --> 00:03:31,975
[SPEAKER_00]: But why do you remember it?

59
00:03:32,355 --> 00:03:33,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they were understood.

60
00:03:33,736 --> 00:03:35,056
[SPEAKER_00]: But why were they understood?

61
00:03:35,636 --> 00:03:35,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

62
00:03:35,997 --> 00:03:37,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the difference then?

63
00:03:37,597 --> 00:03:43,240
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the difference between knowing something, having information about it, and actually understanding it?

64
00:03:44,703 --> 00:03:49,686
[SPEAKER_00]: How you... how you... yeah, the difference is that you... well, the difference is you know it.

65
00:03:50,026 --> 00:03:50,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

66
00:03:50,507 --> 00:03:54,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, understanding means knowing that thing in the nature, not just in the content.

67
00:03:55,850 --> 00:04:00,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding the nature of whatever content you collected through information.

68
00:04:01,534 --> 00:04:04,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like the most general description of what understanding is.

69
00:04:04,475 --> 00:04:08,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you never look for the nature, but you only focus on the content,

70
00:04:09,138 --> 00:04:20,136
[SPEAKER_01]: of your methods, of your techniques, of your motions, of your observances, and then you hope for a new content to be that understanding of the practice and liberation, nibbāna, and it's not.

71
00:04:21,242 --> 00:04:22,663
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha did not use a different word.

72
00:04:22,703 --> 00:04:24,044
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the word for understanding.

73
00:04:24,544 --> 00:04:28,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Same word used when a craftsman understands his craft.

74
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:30,488
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows the principle behind the chariot making.

75
00:04:30,528 --> 00:04:31,348
[SPEAKER_01]: He understands it.

76
00:04:31,729 --> 00:04:33,029
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't need to think about it.

77
00:04:33,069 --> 00:04:35,971
[SPEAKER_01]: He just makes it because he understood that it works like that.

78
00:04:36,512 --> 00:04:37,932
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, that's the result.

79
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:39,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But how does he understand it?

80
00:04:39,714 --> 00:04:41,174
[SPEAKER_01]: By making effort to understand it.

81
00:04:41,795 --> 00:04:42,555
[SPEAKER_01]: If you never make...

82
00:04:42,796 --> 00:04:44,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait, what is the nature of this?

83
00:04:44,757 --> 00:04:45,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I know.

84
00:04:46,018 --> 00:04:47,819
[SPEAKER_01]: I have all the content, all the information.

85
00:04:48,039 --> 00:04:49,400
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is the nature of that?

86
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:51,882
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the characteristics of that nature?

87
00:04:51,902 --> 00:04:52,542
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it for?

88
00:04:52,562 --> 00:04:53,523
[SPEAKER_00]: What is this thing here?

89
00:04:53,563 --> 00:04:54,484
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the purpose?

90
00:04:54,544 --> 00:04:55,424
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my purpose?

91
00:04:55,845 --> 00:04:58,047
[SPEAKER_01]: What is basically behind this content?

92
00:04:58,327 --> 00:04:59,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, sensuality needs to be understood.

93
00:05:00,188 --> 00:05:00,789
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a question.

94
00:05:00,809 --> 00:05:02,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense-desire needs to be understood.

95
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:03,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, sense-desire.

96
00:05:03,191 --> 00:05:04,612
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can relate to sense-desire.

97
00:05:05,673 --> 00:05:07,354
[SPEAKER_01]: I had sense-desires in the past.

98
00:05:07,875 --> 00:05:09,056
[SPEAKER_01]: I have sense-desires now.

99
00:05:09,476 --> 00:05:10,537
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the sense-desires?

100
00:05:10,897 --> 00:05:12,398
[SPEAKER_01]: I say sense-desire in the future.

101
00:05:13,199 --> 00:05:15,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself, am I liable to sense-desire in the future?

102
00:05:15,881 --> 00:05:17,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the sense-desire?

103
00:05:18,461 --> 00:05:23,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, sense-desire is when I go after agreeable objects of my senses.

104
00:05:24,205 --> 00:05:26,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

105
00:05:26,287 --> 00:05:26,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

106
00:05:26,687 --> 00:05:30,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is determined?

107
00:05:31,110 --> 00:05:32,612
[SPEAKER_01]: What has determined that agreeability?

108
00:05:33,152 --> 00:05:35,714
[SPEAKER_01]: What is determined by the agreeability?

109
00:05:37,442 --> 00:05:39,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is agreeable, this is disagreeable.

110
00:05:40,004 --> 00:05:41,825
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how things present themselves, pretty much.

111
00:05:42,425 --> 00:05:43,886
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where is your sense desire, then?

112
00:05:44,006 --> 00:05:45,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it in that agreeable or disagreeable?

113
00:05:46,287 --> 00:05:49,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it in you wanting the agreeable and not wanting the disagreeable?

114
00:05:50,890 --> 00:05:58,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if desire is truly in that thing, in that agreeability or disagreeability, it would be kind of... there would be nothing for you to do.

115
00:05:59,756 --> 00:06:01,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Just having the experience of the sight,

116
00:06:02,606 --> 00:06:05,227
[SPEAKER_01]: of agreeable nature would be enclosed in itself.

117
00:06:05,347 --> 00:06:06,708
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be the agreeable experience.

118
00:06:07,168 --> 00:06:10,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't need or you wouldn't be able to want more of it.

119
00:06:11,330 --> 00:06:15,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any notion between agreeable and more of it is already contained within the sight.

120
00:06:17,153 --> 00:06:19,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no room there for self-awareness.

121
00:06:19,274 --> 00:06:20,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want this on account of that.

122
00:06:22,276 --> 00:06:24,437
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, when you have agreeable sight, you want more of it.

123
00:06:25,637 --> 00:06:26,658
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to touch it as well.

124
00:06:27,018 --> 00:06:28,359
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to taste it, if it's edible.

125
00:06:29,222 --> 00:06:29,863
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to smell it.

126
00:06:31,024 --> 00:06:31,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, why is that?

127
00:06:31,824 --> 00:06:32,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just a sight.

128
00:06:33,126 --> 00:06:34,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how does sight make you want to smell?

129
00:06:34,607 --> 00:06:37,270
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's you who wants to do all of that on top of it.

130
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:42,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize, okay, so sense desire is not in beautiful things in the world.

131
00:06:42,154 --> 00:06:46,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense desire is in my attitude towards beautiful things in the world.

132
00:06:46,858 --> 00:06:50,302
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I can't just help myself, but automatically go towards it.

133
00:06:50,882 --> 00:06:52,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Same like moth sees the light.

134
00:06:53,168 --> 00:06:54,429
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't think, just goes straight.

135
00:06:55,310 --> 00:06:56,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't help himself.

136
00:06:56,351 --> 00:06:58,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still master's responsibility, by the way.

137
00:07:01,976 --> 00:07:06,340
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that the light makes you, commands you, controls you, takes over your actions.

138
00:07:07,321 --> 00:07:08,702
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you just can't help yourself.

139
00:07:10,784 --> 00:07:14,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you realize, oh, you're already now practicing understanding.

140
00:07:15,702 --> 00:07:20,204
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I understand that sensual pleasures, that sensual desire...

141
00:07:20,224 --> 00:07:24,145
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understand what sensual desire is... Well, you know the escape.

142
00:07:24,345 --> 00:07:26,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you will... the escape will become apparent.

143
00:07:27,546 --> 00:07:28,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the escape?

144
00:07:28,987 --> 00:07:29,647
[SPEAKER_01]: From sensuality.

145
00:07:29,907 --> 00:07:34,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's exactly... it's not... it's removal of the assumption that sensual pleasures are in things.

146
00:07:36,590 --> 00:07:39,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the first... the basis for any... any escape.

147
00:07:40,312 --> 00:07:45,334
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you don't escape it... the only reason you're still entangled is because you don't see where the trap is.

148
00:07:45,494 --> 00:07:46,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you step in it.

149
00:07:48,616 --> 00:07:53,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you learn what's really the trap and what it isn't, then you know how to not step in it.

150
00:07:56,079 --> 00:07:59,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the trap is your own desire that you assume to be in those things.

151
00:08:00,502 --> 00:08:03,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And by not seeing where it actually is, you're fully already trapped.

152
00:08:05,494 --> 00:08:08,196
[SPEAKER_01]: By not seeing that it's your attitude that's the root of your desire.

153
00:08:10,338 --> 00:08:11,178
[SPEAKER_00]: What about the pressure?

154
00:08:12,539 --> 00:08:17,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, pressure, see, pressure is kind of secondary, so to speak.

155
00:08:19,705 --> 00:08:31,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the experience of, say, agreeable sight, you are pressured because you have been going after the agreeability of your senses by default.

156
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:44,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contact, passa or pressure, whichever way you want to translate it, has been maintained through you ignoring where the desire is.

157
00:08:45,946 --> 00:08:57,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's even worse because there is no first point in our experience as a whole, in the entire saṃsāra, before which you did not ignore things and then you started ignoring.

158
00:08:57,611 --> 00:09:01,673
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no first point before which you were not pressured and then you became pressured.

159
00:09:02,957 --> 00:09:05,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although pressure is secondary, it was always first.

160
00:09:06,699 --> 00:09:09,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is secondary, yet it was always first.

161
00:09:10,101 --> 00:09:11,942
[SPEAKER_01]: By not knowing that, that's why it was first.

162
00:09:13,863 --> 00:09:21,248
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why there is no discernible point, manifestation, first moment when ignorance manifests, when avijjā manifests, as the Buddha described it.

163
00:09:22,669 --> 00:09:27,792
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you realize that, oh, the only reason I'm pressured is because I've been giving in to the pressure.

164
00:09:29,108 --> 00:09:38,213
[SPEAKER_01]: If I stop giving in to the pressure for agreeable, pressure for getting rid of the disagreeable, that pressure will fade, because I'm not fueling it anymore.

165
00:09:38,753 --> 00:09:45,396
[SPEAKER_01]: If the pressure fades, any assumption that you jumped into by giving in to the pressure will have to disappear as well.

166
00:09:45,757 --> 00:09:52,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get to see, ah, sense desires were always unpleasant.

167
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:53,521
[SPEAKER_01]: They will always be unpleasant.

168
00:09:53,761 --> 00:09:54,821
[SPEAKER_01]: There was never anything different.

169
00:09:54,841 --> 00:09:56,042
[SPEAKER_01]: It was me misperceiving them.

170
00:09:57,030 --> 00:10:03,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the man affected with leprosy, burning embers are providing him pleasure, although they're not.

171
00:10:03,674 --> 00:10:07,656
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he realizes these burning embers were always uncomfortable and painful.

172
00:10:07,896 --> 00:10:08,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

173
00:10:08,776 --> 00:10:15,440
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because of my wrong view, misperception, I was regarding that genuine sharp pain as pleasant.

174
00:10:18,381 --> 00:10:20,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of this already are.

175
00:10:20,162 --> 00:10:21,443
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you understand things.

176
00:10:22,485 --> 00:10:27,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Questioning, looking for the nature, discerning the characteristics, questioning further.

177
00:10:28,269 --> 00:10:28,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

178
00:10:28,889 --> 00:10:29,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Day and night.

179
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:31,651
[SPEAKER_01]: First watch of the night, second watch of the night.

180
00:10:31,811 --> 00:10:35,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Just purifying, as the Buddha said, his mind from the unwholesome state.

181
00:10:35,973 --> 00:10:37,714
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the fundamental unwholesome state?

182
00:10:38,375 --> 00:10:39,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Lack of understanding is.

183
00:10:40,596 --> 00:10:41,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance is.

184
00:10:42,157 --> 00:10:44,078
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the root of all unwholesome.

185
00:10:45,499 --> 00:10:50,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you purify your mind by removing your own self-induced ignoring of the nature of things.

186
00:10:52,205 --> 00:10:53,606
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of aversion?

187
00:10:54,426 --> 00:10:55,387
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of anger?

188
00:10:56,308 --> 00:10:57,368
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of choice?

189
00:10:59,550 --> 00:11:01,731
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of feeling?

190
00:11:02,612 --> 00:11:03,812
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of perceiving?

191
00:11:04,573 --> 00:11:05,814
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of it?

192
00:11:06,494 --> 00:11:08,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just what is perceiving, what is feeling.

193
00:11:08,756 --> 00:11:09,856
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of it?

194
00:11:10,477 --> 00:11:18,702
[SPEAKER_01]: What is a universal characteristic of feeling that is present there as its nature, regardless of what is being specifically felt?

195
00:11:20,336 --> 00:11:22,436
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the universal nature of perception?

196
00:11:22,937 --> 00:11:26,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of what sense is perceiving and what is being perceived through that sense.

197
00:11:32,579 --> 00:11:40,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, just on the... what is... okay, sorry...

198
00:11:46,391 --> 00:11:48,613
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the cessation of sensual pleasures?

199
00:11:49,653 --> 00:11:51,034
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cessation of it?

200
00:11:51,074 --> 00:11:52,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of desire, the delight.

201
00:11:53,256 --> 00:11:55,757
[SPEAKER_00]: It says here, with cessation of contact.

202
00:11:55,777 --> 00:11:57,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of pressure.

203
00:11:57,258 --> 00:12:02,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of... with cessation of pressure, contact, is the cessation of sensual pleasures.

204
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:02,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

205
00:12:03,362 --> 00:12:03,923
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said.

206
00:12:04,003 --> 00:12:04,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

207
00:12:04,483 --> 00:12:12,649
[SPEAKER_01]: If you if you stop habitually going after the agreeable, you are inadvertently not fueling the pressure anymore.

208
00:12:12,709 --> 00:12:14,370
[SPEAKER_01]: You... By not fueling the pressure,

209
00:12:16,007 --> 00:12:17,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be pressured to act anymore.

210
00:12:17,728 --> 00:12:19,149
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the diminishing of pressure.

211
00:12:19,189 --> 00:12:19,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

212
00:12:19,589 --> 00:12:20,269
[SPEAKER_01]: It diminishes.

213
00:12:20,389 --> 00:12:24,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through developing understanding and seeing clearly, the pressure will fade away.

214
00:12:25,812 --> 00:12:34,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation, fading away of pressure, is then cessation, fading away of the necessary basis for sense desires.

215
00:12:34,917 --> 00:12:37,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have a desire without implicit pressure?

216
00:12:39,219 --> 00:12:39,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

217
00:12:39,739 --> 00:12:41,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, where does the pressure come from?

218
00:12:41,896 --> 00:12:42,957
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just asking a different way.

219
00:12:43,077 --> 00:12:45,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you want to ask really...

220
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:49,302
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't come from, as in it comes and now it's here.

221
00:12:49,783 --> 00:12:52,225
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there because that which determines it is there.

222
00:12:53,206 --> 00:12:54,927
[SPEAKER_01]: The saṅkhara of the pressure is there.

223
00:12:55,608 --> 00:13:01,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is determining or where... how does... how is pressure maintained?

224
00:13:02,774 --> 00:13:05,496
[SPEAKER_01]: What is its... What is the necessary basis for pressure?

225
00:13:06,197 --> 00:13:08,739
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a perception of agreeable and disagreeable.

226
00:13:10,398 --> 00:13:13,821
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis is the perception of...

227
00:13:15,603 --> 00:13:18,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the necessary base of perception, if you're listening really well?

228
00:13:18,665 --> 00:13:19,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Senses.

229
00:13:20,206 --> 00:13:23,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes, nose, ears, body, mouth.

230
00:13:24,170 --> 00:13:26,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, what's the necessary base for senses?

231
00:13:26,852 --> 00:13:30,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can go further now, but it's going to become too abstract for people.

232
00:13:31,596 --> 00:13:33,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's stick with what you can relate to.

233
00:13:33,938 --> 00:13:34,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Senses.

234
00:13:35,980 --> 00:13:40,444
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you have perception of agreeable or disagreeable if you don't have the eyes?

235
00:13:41,485 --> 00:13:41,565
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

236
00:13:42,986 --> 00:13:43,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

237
00:13:43,807 --> 00:13:49,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the necessary basis for your loves and your hates is being able to see.

238
00:13:51,434 --> 00:13:53,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, that's not how you regard your loves and your hates.

239
00:13:54,336 --> 00:13:55,417
[SPEAKER_01]: They're far more important.

240
00:13:55,838 --> 00:13:56,658
[SPEAKER_01]: They're put first.

241
00:13:57,479 --> 00:14:03,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Although they're always within the domain of sight and eyes,

242
00:14:04,067 --> 00:14:06,028
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually more important to you.

243
00:14:06,768 --> 00:14:10,269
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that as more fundamental, what I love and what I hate.

244
00:14:12,570 --> 00:14:20,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've been ignoring the right, the nature of that relationship for so long, that now you don't even see that it's actually the other way around.

245
00:14:20,813 --> 00:14:22,934
[SPEAKER_01]: You turn the whole picture upside down.

246
00:14:22,954 --> 00:14:23,754
[SPEAKER_00]: That is dependent.

247
00:14:23,774 --> 00:14:25,535
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is a second, you put first.

248
00:14:25,695 --> 00:14:29,916
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is dependent, you put as the kind of fundamental determinant.

249
00:14:30,817 --> 00:14:32,037
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's actually being determined.

250
00:14:34,233 --> 00:14:39,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is determining that pressure again?

251
00:14:39,678 --> 00:14:41,640
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the fact that you have eyes and senses.

252
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:43,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not enough in itself.

253
00:14:44,202 --> 00:14:48,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that's still fairly abstract, would probably be for most people.

254
00:14:49,187 --> 00:14:54,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you want to dwell on the nature of that saṅkhara, of eyes, of the sixth sense space.

255
00:14:55,425 --> 00:14:58,227
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature now of those eyes?

256
00:14:58,308 --> 00:14:59,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Connect with it, so to speak.

257
00:15:00,089 --> 00:15:00,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand it.

258
00:15:01,831 --> 00:15:05,874
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, everything else that's being determined by it cannot remain indifferent to it.

259
00:15:06,194 --> 00:15:09,117
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be affected by that understanding.

260
00:15:09,257 --> 00:15:10,638
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the nature of your senses?

261
00:15:10,979 --> 00:15:11,979
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of perception?

262
00:15:13,721 --> 00:15:14,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you in control?

263
00:15:16,133 --> 00:15:21,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, but as you're saying, that's, that's, you should go deep, you go deeper in the questioning, you go deeper.

264
00:15:21,999 --> 00:15:32,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anyway, it started off with sensuality, questioning sensuality, that it's... but then it goes to questioning feelings and then questioning perceptions.

265
00:15:32,770 --> 00:15:36,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the nature of your position, nature of your feelings, then it's questioning the nature of your perceptions.

266
00:15:39,333 --> 00:15:42,115
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... just questioning sensuality...

267
00:15:42,135 --> 00:15:43,356
[SPEAKER_01]: It will take you everywhere, of course.

268
00:15:43,376 --> 00:15:44,136
[SPEAKER_01]: ...through the whole thing.

269
00:15:44,156 --> 00:15:44,376
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

270
00:15:46,198 --> 00:15:53,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anyway, let's go on to feelings.

271
00:15:55,404 --> 00:15:56,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, feelings should be understood.

272
00:16:01,968 --> 00:16:03,710
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the source and origin of feelings?

273
00:16:05,691 --> 00:16:08,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure is the source and origin of feelings.

274
00:16:08,797 --> 00:16:09,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:16:09,858 --> 00:16:10,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure.

276
00:16:13,099 --> 00:16:15,160
[SPEAKER_00]: That is why it is felt.

277
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:19,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Something is felt because of that pressure.

278
00:16:20,462 --> 00:16:20,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

279
00:16:20,722 --> 00:16:21,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is already felt.

280
00:16:22,883 --> 00:16:24,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is what's felt.

281
00:16:24,704 --> 00:16:25,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is the pressure.

282
00:16:26,065 --> 00:16:26,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

283
00:16:26,865 --> 00:16:31,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the cessation of pressure is a cessation of sensual pleasures, but also...

284
00:16:31,948 --> 00:16:34,149
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a cessation of that misperception.

285
00:16:35,089 --> 00:16:36,570
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a cessation of feeling.

286
00:16:36,770 --> 00:16:36,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

287
00:16:37,943 --> 00:16:46,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not only that pressure ceases or diminishes, that feeling diminishes.

288
00:16:46,890 --> 00:16:46,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

289
00:16:48,592 --> 00:16:50,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing.

290
00:16:50,814 --> 00:17:00,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable, disagreeable, that is felt in regard to perception, in regard to what's perceived, that's in itself.

291
00:17:00,642 --> 00:17:02,343
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing...

292
00:17:02,403 --> 00:17:04,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Disagreeable does not bother you in itself.

293
00:17:05,413 --> 00:17:08,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable does not pull you towards it in itself either.

294
00:17:10,758 --> 00:17:16,362
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's you being pressured by it, means you're feeling it, means you are pulled, you are being repelled and so on.

295
00:17:17,483 --> 00:17:27,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, removal, cessation of that pressure, through clear understanding, will make you then not feel disagreeable as pain.

296
00:17:28,612 --> 00:17:33,216
[SPEAKER_01]: It will then make you not feel the agreeable as sensual delight for more of it.

297
00:17:34,309 --> 00:17:37,612
[SPEAKER_01]: It will make you not feel the neutral feeling unpleasantly.

298
00:17:43,016 --> 00:17:45,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of pressure through understanding.

299
00:17:49,302 --> 00:17:49,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

300
00:17:49,762 --> 00:17:53,125
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way for pressure to cease is to understand the nature of it.

301
00:17:53,885 --> 00:17:57,909
[SPEAKER_01]: To not put it first, but see it as determined by something else that's not in your control.

302
00:17:58,850 --> 00:18:01,412
[SPEAKER_00]: We also spoke recently about the non-reactive

303
00:18:03,452 --> 00:18:10,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have to have a non-reactive attitude, as in not jumping into pleasure, not trying to deny the pain.

304
00:18:11,039 --> 00:18:12,340
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only way you can understand it.

305
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:15,683
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only way how you're going to be able to allow it to endure for what it is.

306
00:18:15,743 --> 00:18:17,104
[SPEAKER_00]: For what it is, so that you can understand.

307
00:18:17,124 --> 00:18:18,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's clear, without you...

308
00:18:18,686 --> 00:18:19,627
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a prerequisite.

309
00:18:20,027 --> 00:18:26,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you not reacting out of pressure will not necessarily result in you understanding pressure.

310
00:18:27,257 --> 00:18:31,059
[SPEAKER_01]: But certainly, there can be no understanding unless you stop reacting first.

311
00:18:31,519 --> 00:18:31,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

312
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:34,981
[SPEAKER_00]: But those those two things are non-reaction.

313
00:18:37,102 --> 00:18:41,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And... Non-reaction will make you then... will make you endure it.

314
00:18:43,066 --> 00:18:46,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Having something endure means becoming more familiar with it, whether you want it or not.

315
00:18:46,988 --> 00:18:47,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

316
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:48,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, but now...

317
00:18:48,520 --> 00:18:58,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Being more familiar with it means, oh, you can look closer and closer and get to understand it without just habitually, reactively trying to either have more of it or less of it or ignore it.

318
00:18:59,726 --> 00:19:03,228
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing becomes clearer and you can see it in detail.

319
00:19:03,248 --> 00:19:07,150
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why people don't understand the nature of sensuality is because by default you go to it.

320
00:19:07,971 --> 00:19:12,513
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why people don't understand the nature of ill will and anger is by default you act out of it.

321
00:19:13,914 --> 00:19:14,835
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to stop doing that.

322
00:19:14,855 --> 00:19:15,615
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not optional.

323
00:19:16,878 --> 00:19:18,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Or neutral feeling, you close.

324
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:23,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral feeling, you basically want to get rid of it by distracting yourself from it, because it's just too unbearable.

325
00:19:24,444 --> 00:19:25,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Try and hide from it.

326
00:19:25,485 --> 00:19:26,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Try and hide from it, yeah.

327
00:19:27,026 --> 00:19:27,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Try and replace it.

328
00:19:32,110 --> 00:19:35,873
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the result of feelings?

329
00:19:38,081 --> 00:19:40,883
[SPEAKER_01]: What choices you make on account of it.

330
00:19:41,003 --> 00:19:42,544
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer here, yeah.

331
00:19:43,264 --> 00:19:46,726
[SPEAKER_00]: One produces an individual existence.

332
00:19:47,767 --> 00:19:48,847
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a weird situation.

333
00:19:49,328 --> 00:19:56,132
[SPEAKER_00]: That corresponds with whatever feelings one experiences and which may be the consequence either of merit or demerit.

334
00:19:56,912 --> 00:19:57,532
[SPEAKER_00]: One chooses.

335
00:19:57,833 --> 00:19:58,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, yeah.

336
00:19:58,913 --> 00:19:59,594
[SPEAKER_00]: One's choice.

337
00:19:59,614 --> 00:20:00,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Makes a decision.

338
00:20:00,194 --> 00:20:00,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

339
00:20:00,514 --> 00:20:04,737
[SPEAKER_01]: One chooses and acts out of that choice, is the result of the feelings.

340
00:20:04,777 --> 00:20:04,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

341
00:20:06,620 --> 00:20:11,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Which obviously can be meritorious, demeritorious, or neither meritorious nor non-meritorious.

342
00:20:13,272 --> 00:20:14,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Like neutral action, neither good nor bad.

343
00:20:14,872 --> 00:20:15,993
[SPEAKER_00]: By acting out of feelings.

344
00:20:16,953 --> 00:20:17,893
[SPEAKER_01]: One performs action.

345
00:20:18,573 --> 00:20:19,533
[SPEAKER_01]: One decides action.

346
00:20:19,573 --> 00:20:23,034
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, every action is all intention already.

347
00:20:23,094 --> 00:20:23,794
[SPEAKER_00]: One is creating.

348
00:20:23,834 --> 00:20:26,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Means your choice, your choice is already your kamma.

349
00:20:26,815 --> 00:20:27,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

350
00:20:27,575 --> 00:20:36,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can choose to act further out of that choice, which is another set of choices on top of your choices, but even your fundamental choice, it's already your kamma, it's already an action.

351
00:20:37,778 --> 00:20:38,938
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you remove the pressure

352
00:20:39,438 --> 00:20:42,121
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the necessary basis for acting out of pressure.

353
00:20:42,181 --> 00:20:45,285
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you act out of pressure if there is no pressure felt?

354
00:20:45,365 --> 00:20:46,647
[SPEAKER_00]: There's the ending of kāma there.

355
00:20:46,667 --> 00:20:47,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Ending of kāma there and then.

356
00:20:47,888 --> 00:20:50,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait for the saṃsāra to sort of...

357
00:20:51,325 --> 00:20:52,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Run out, or something like that.

358
00:20:52,886 --> 00:21:02,270
[SPEAKER_01]: You ended it by stepping outside of it, by not feeling it anymore, by not partaking in it anymore, by not deciding upon it anymore, by not perceiving it anymore, misperceiving it anymore.

359
00:21:02,290 --> 00:21:03,731
[SPEAKER_00]: By not reacting and understanding.

360
00:21:03,751 --> 00:21:04,491
[SPEAKER_01]: By not reacting, yeah.

361
00:21:04,891 --> 00:21:06,452
[SPEAKER_01]: By not reacting, you get to understand it.

362
00:21:06,572 --> 00:21:09,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you understood it, well, then reacting becomes impossible.

363
00:21:15,516 --> 00:21:17,757
[SPEAKER_00]: It continues about perceptions.

364
00:21:19,257 --> 00:21:20,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceptions should be understood.

365
00:21:25,580 --> 00:21:28,661
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the source and origin of perceptions?

366
00:21:29,641 --> 00:21:34,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you said before, contact, pressure, is the source of perceptions.

367
00:21:34,922 --> 00:21:37,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, of the perceptions.

368
00:21:37,103 --> 00:21:43,485
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, there is no first point when these perceptions became bad and misperceived.

369
00:21:43,945 --> 00:21:45,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Your perception is misperception.

370
00:21:45,985 --> 00:21:46,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

371
00:21:46,646 --> 00:21:48,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Your feeling is misfeeling, so to speak.

372
00:21:49,446 --> 00:21:50,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Your intention is misintention.

373
00:21:50,987 --> 00:21:51,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's misfelt.

374
00:21:52,544 --> 00:21:59,928
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's only like... you can only call it misperception, misfeeling and misintention from the arahant's point of view.

375
00:22:00,768 --> 00:22:03,350
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you have to be one or the other either.

376
00:22:03,430 --> 00:22:10,713
[SPEAKER_01]: We're not talking about theory, in which case, yes, it's true to say that these perceptions don't exist in an arahant and so on.

377
00:22:11,434 --> 00:22:14,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you don't start from a neutral position.

378
00:22:14,635 --> 00:22:19,758
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't start by... somebody who's affected with ignorance cannot start from a position that can understand arahant.

379
00:22:23,130 --> 00:22:29,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any perception that you can have is the misperception resulting out of pressure.

380
00:22:30,435 --> 00:22:34,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Any feeling that you have is basically a misfeeling resulting out of pressure.

381
00:22:34,238 --> 00:22:39,301
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any choice that you have, any intention that you have, it's a misintention resulting out of pressure.

382
00:22:39,962 --> 00:22:44,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, then, the Buddha would say, when he was teaching somebody who is not an Arahant yet,

383
00:22:45,125 --> 00:22:47,486
[SPEAKER_01]: He would call the cessation of the five aggregates.

384
00:22:47,926 --> 00:22:52,607
[SPEAKER_01]: He would say feeling ceases, perception ceases, intention ceases.

385
00:22:52,627 --> 00:22:52,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

386
00:22:53,087 --> 00:22:53,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they do.

387
00:22:54,747 --> 00:23:00,488
[SPEAKER_01]: From that point of view, those type of feeling, intention, perception, rooted in pressure, they're gone.

388
00:23:00,868 --> 00:23:07,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's true to say that those type of... those feeling, perception, intention, they don't exist anymore in anārānta.

389
00:23:08,890 --> 00:23:11,652
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not to say that arahant is sort of like a...

390
00:23:11,712 --> 00:23:12,553
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no perception.

391
00:23:12,573 --> 00:23:14,434
[SPEAKER_01]: ...robot who doesn't perceive anything.

392
00:23:14,454 --> 00:23:19,658
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just that the perception that he has, the only way to relate to it is to become an arahant yourself.

393
00:23:20,119 --> 00:23:27,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot understand it even theoretically, because theoretical understanding is still affected by your misperception rooted in the pressure.

394
00:23:27,744 --> 00:23:27,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

395
00:23:29,025 --> 00:23:31,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, it begins with perception needs to be understood.

396
00:23:32,328 --> 00:23:38,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, because I don't understand, thus... By understanding the perception, you will uproot the pressure

397
00:23:38,769 --> 00:23:40,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And the perception that was being determined by that pressure.

398
00:23:41,730 --> 00:23:42,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what I mean.

399
00:23:42,410 --> 00:23:50,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, speaking in that kind of, like, say, a theoretical model, perception comes first.

400
00:23:50,874 --> 00:23:51,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling comes first.

401
00:23:52,034 --> 00:23:53,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Intention comes first.

402
00:23:53,175 --> 00:23:54,135
[SPEAKER_01]: They're the aggregates there.

403
00:23:54,895 --> 00:23:58,136
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pressure in the five aggregates.

404
00:23:58,757 --> 00:24:00,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be.

405
00:24:00,517 --> 00:24:02,258
[SPEAKER_01]: They're like five heaps exist on their own.

406
00:24:02,698 --> 00:24:04,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Arise and fall, as the Buddha said.

407
00:24:05,839 --> 00:24:07,240
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not your starting position.

408
00:24:08,922 --> 00:24:17,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Your starting position is that all of those aggregates, that are independent of you and of each other, are taken up by the pressure, by the assumption.

409
00:24:18,228 --> 00:24:23,932
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, by taking them up means you put second as first.

410
00:24:24,372 --> 00:24:31,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pressure, which is really dependent only because there are things that can pressure you, like perception, feeling, intentions,

411
00:24:32,600 --> 00:24:34,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, can you be pressured if there is no perception?

412
00:24:34,822 --> 00:24:36,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be pressured if there's nothing felt?

413
00:24:36,903 --> 00:24:38,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be pressured if there are no intentions?

414
00:24:39,545 --> 00:24:40,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

415
00:24:40,887 --> 00:24:45,810
[SPEAKER_01]: If there are no ways of manifesting pressure, there can be no pressure manifesting.

416
00:24:46,651 --> 00:24:47,352
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

417
00:24:48,673 --> 00:24:50,694
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you put that pressure first.

418
00:24:50,894 --> 00:24:55,338
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although that pressure is determined by the ability, basically, to be pressured, so to speak,

419
00:24:56,026 --> 00:25:05,056
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume that pressure is first, so you keep acting out of pressure, and whenever you act out of pressure, you assume pressure first, and then everything else that you act on account of is second.

420
00:25:05,877 --> 00:25:13,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anything you perceive, anything you feel, anything you intend, is actually made subordinate, by your own view, to the pressure.

421
00:25:14,226 --> 00:25:14,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

422
00:25:14,486 --> 00:25:17,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the ultimate goal is getting rid of the discomfort of the pressure.

423
00:25:19,490 --> 00:25:21,631
[SPEAKER_01]: But this comfort of the pressure, which is what?

424
00:25:22,131 --> 00:25:22,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you created it?

425
00:25:23,012 --> 00:25:23,152
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

426
00:25:23,612 --> 00:25:24,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you subjected to it?

427
00:25:24,492 --> 00:25:25,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have arisen on its own?

428
00:25:25,753 --> 00:25:25,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

429
00:25:26,353 --> 00:25:26,573
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

430
00:25:26,974 --> 00:25:29,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, through what I perceived, through what I felt, through what I intended.

431
00:25:31,316 --> 00:25:32,656
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's like a vicious circle.

432
00:25:35,838 --> 00:25:38,879
[SPEAKER_01]: You create the pressure by not understanding perception, feeling, intention.

433
00:25:39,120 --> 00:25:39,840
[SPEAKER_01]: You are pressured.

434
00:25:41,363 --> 00:25:53,031
[SPEAKER_01]: But by being pressured and acting out of pressure, you then maintain perception, feeling, intention, that fuel, that provide the basis for pressure, that makes them more, that makes the basis bigger, and that's how pressure proliferates and grows.

435
00:25:53,692 --> 00:25:55,733
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the upādāna.

436
00:25:55,753 --> 00:26:04,039
[SPEAKER_01]: The more fuel for the fire there is, the bigger the fire, the more fuel is consumed, the more fuel is found, the bigger the fire grows.

437
00:26:05,020 --> 00:26:06,741
[SPEAKER_01]: But fire is not the fuel.

438
00:26:08,335 --> 00:26:13,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Upādāna is not in the aggregates, as the Buddha said, but he said there is no upādāna anywhere apart from the aggregates.

439
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:27,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire and lust in regard to the aggregates, as in acting out of the value that your desire must be satisfied, desire to achieve, desire to get rid of, makes everything else subordinate to that desire, although desire is second to everything else.

440
00:26:28,177 --> 00:26:30,099
[SPEAKER_01]: You made it first and now everything else is second.

441
00:26:30,740 --> 00:26:39,248
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of these aggregates as second, perception as second, feeling as second, intentions as second, will cease once you remove the pressure.

442
00:26:41,690 --> 00:26:48,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you have them as second and you don't make them, you don't understand them, they will keep fueling the pressure, they will keep taking itself as first.

443
00:26:54,415 --> 00:26:58,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I said, like, you are pressured because you perceive and you feel and you intend.

444
00:27:00,220 --> 00:27:02,982
[SPEAKER_01]: But the perception, feeling and intention are that pressure.

445
00:27:03,042 --> 00:27:06,024
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how pressure manifests.

446
00:27:06,304 --> 00:27:10,708
[SPEAKER_00]: I got that image of... it's... you have a twisted view of things.

447
00:27:10,748 --> 00:27:14,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Like upside-down, that the whole... everything's the wrong way around.

448
00:27:14,491 --> 00:27:14,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

449
00:27:14,911 --> 00:27:16,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Or twisted out of shape.

450
00:27:16,292 --> 00:27:16,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

451
00:27:18,093 --> 00:27:18,974
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the pressure.

452
00:27:19,254 --> 00:27:19,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that... Yeah.

453
00:27:20,255 --> 00:27:22,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all those twisted shapes will cease.

454
00:27:22,317 --> 00:27:22,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

455
00:27:23,426 --> 00:27:25,988
[SPEAKER_00]: And when things turn right, play around, pop away.

456
00:27:26,008 --> 00:27:26,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

457
00:27:26,248 --> 00:27:28,609
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove that force that's been twisting them.

458
00:27:29,230 --> 00:27:29,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

459
00:27:30,551 --> 00:27:31,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not bent out of shape.

460
00:27:31,832 --> 00:27:32,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

461
00:27:32,152 --> 00:27:34,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like your aggregates get replaced by something.

462
00:27:34,974 --> 00:27:35,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Aggregates remain aggregates.

463
00:27:36,034 --> 00:27:39,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorant aggregates, arahants, aggregates, aggregates are there.

464
00:27:40,477 --> 00:27:42,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings, their perceptions, their intentions, there.

465
00:27:42,779 --> 00:27:44,600
[SPEAKER_01]: But, now they're seen for what they are.

466
00:27:45,501 --> 00:27:46,622
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not put second.

467
00:27:46,882 --> 00:27:47,822
[SPEAKER_01]: They're seen as first.

468
00:27:47,842 --> 00:27:48,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

469
00:28:01,586 --> 00:28:10,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, what is the cessation of perceptions, cessation of... what is the cessation of misperception?

470
00:28:10,174 --> 00:28:21,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, I don't want to call it perception, feeling, intention, because by saying misperception, allows person who hasn't understood perception, feeling and intention, to think they can pertain to understanding of correct

471
00:28:22,738 --> 00:28:27,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So, cessation of feeling, intention and perception for an ignorant mind is achieved through cessation of the pressure of ignorant mind.

472
00:28:27,259 --> 00:28:29,260
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you free yourself from pressure?

473
00:28:53,427 --> 00:28:53,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding.

474
00:28:54,487 --> 00:28:54,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

475
00:28:54,847 --> 00:28:55,927
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you arrive at understanding?

476
00:28:59,528 --> 00:29:00,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to understand.

477
00:29:01,108 --> 00:29:04,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but what is the necessary basis for you to try to understand correctly?

478
00:29:05,529 --> 00:29:06,309
[SPEAKER_00]: That restraint.

479
00:29:06,609 --> 00:29:06,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

480
00:29:06,869 --> 00:29:07,930
[SPEAKER_01]: By not acting out of pressure.

481
00:29:08,150 --> 00:29:09,190
[SPEAKER_00]: And not reacting as I said.

482
00:29:09,210 --> 00:29:09,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

483
00:29:10,150 --> 00:29:12,891
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I just reiterated in the same, because it's not an easy topic.

484
00:29:12,951 --> 00:29:13,811
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a tricky question.

485
00:29:14,051 --> 00:29:14,611
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

486
00:29:15,031 --> 00:29:16,291
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't act out of pressure.

487
00:29:17,651 --> 00:29:20,972
[SPEAKER_01]: See, even you haven't understood the pressure, but inadvertently,

488
00:29:21,447 --> 00:29:27,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking it on faith that you shouldn't act out of pressure, you're ceasing to provide the basis for proliferation of that pressure.

489
00:29:28,716 --> 00:29:32,501
[SPEAKER_01]: You're ceasing to provide the basis for that misperception, misfeeling and misintention.

490
00:29:32,521 --> 00:29:36,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And you think, oh, there is the real intention, real perception.

491
00:29:37,329 --> 00:29:38,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Theoretically, there is.

492
00:29:38,390 --> 00:29:39,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically, there isn't.

493
00:29:39,470 --> 00:29:40,791
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not my concern right now.

494
00:29:40,911 --> 00:29:58,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Your concern is not cultivate the pressure, action out of pressure anymore, understand the pressure, and then, that feeling, perception, intention that are left, after you're free from pressure, then you wouldn't call it perception, feeling, intention, if those are the designations used for the ones that were pressured in the past.

495
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:02,921
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why five aggregates cease.

496
00:30:03,041 --> 00:30:06,502
[SPEAKER_01]: For an arahant, the puthujjana's five aggregates have ceased completely.

497
00:30:07,023 --> 00:30:10,144
[SPEAKER_01]: But from puthujjana's point of view, that means, oh, this is like dhyate, nothingness.

498
00:30:10,204 --> 00:30:11,424
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people would freak out.

499
00:30:12,045 --> 00:30:15,326
[SPEAKER_01]: When they would get the sense what the Buddha is teaching, so they will kill us.

500
00:30:15,346 --> 00:30:16,587
[SPEAKER_01]: They will make me disappear.

501
00:30:17,187 --> 00:30:21,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any relation they can have with the idea of themself is the idea of these misperceived things.

502
00:30:22,329 --> 00:30:24,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are put second while they're actually first.

503
00:30:25,898 --> 00:30:34,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have a... hold on... if you have a recognition that, oh, that would be removed, then you think, I will be removed, and that freaks you out.

504
00:30:36,602 --> 00:30:37,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But why would you be doing that?

505
00:30:38,562 --> 00:30:39,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you freak out?

506
00:30:39,983 --> 00:30:43,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because you want to get rid of that discomfort which pressures you.

507
00:30:43,784 --> 00:30:45,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Both down to the same principle.

508
00:30:46,739 --> 00:30:48,460
[SPEAKER_00]: And really, that's what you want to be free of.

509
00:30:48,480 --> 00:30:51,543
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be suffering.

510
00:30:51,583 --> 00:30:52,603
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be free of suffering.

511
00:30:52,623 --> 00:31:00,909
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, when you ask, when someone asks or is concerned with the state of an arahant, it's irrelevant.

512
00:31:01,310 --> 00:31:02,490
[SPEAKER_00]: So, who cares?

513
00:31:02,671 --> 00:31:08,835
[SPEAKER_01]: It's relevant if you're concerned on the level of collecting information, so you can clarify what your goal should be.

514
00:31:08,855 --> 00:31:09,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

515
00:31:09,435 --> 00:31:12,918
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're concerned on the level of satisfying the craving for my answer,

516
00:31:13,480 --> 00:31:17,810
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just acting out of pressure, so no amount of knowledge of an arahant will make you anything closer to him.

517
00:31:18,231 --> 00:31:22,320
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why if you start thinking, hold on, I can get all the knowledge about an arahant.

518
00:31:23,422 --> 00:31:27,004
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to understand an arahant, is to become at least a sotāpanna.

519
00:31:27,745 --> 00:31:29,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Preferably, become an arahant myself.

520
00:31:30,106 --> 00:31:33,889
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only way you can understand an arahant.

521
00:31:34,029 --> 00:31:36,650
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's your goal, then, then you'll start looking in the right direction.

522
00:31:36,670 --> 00:31:44,295
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying, you should not think, you are practicing the dhamma, you can practice the dhamma, unless you're at least a sotāpanna.

523
00:31:46,288 --> 00:31:50,150
[SPEAKER_01]: You should think that I can practice the dhamma if I become a sotāpanna.

524
00:31:50,170 --> 00:31:51,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, that's my goal.

525
00:31:51,911 --> 00:32:00,635
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you think, I'm not a sotāpanna, but I can practice the dhamma, means you allow yourself that kind of space in between for which you have no measure.

526
00:32:01,256 --> 00:32:04,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only measure of the dhamma is knowing what dhamma is, which is sotāpatti.

527
00:32:05,558 --> 00:32:07,299
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said countless amount of time.

528
00:32:07,899 --> 00:32:14,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet here you are, know that you don't know what the dhamma is, but at the same time refuse to give up the idea that you are practicing the dhamma, that

529
00:32:16,815 --> 00:32:23,001
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you will not push yourself in the right direction, whereby there will be no compromise in between.

530
00:32:23,121 --> 00:32:23,241
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

531
00:32:23,341 --> 00:32:25,143
[SPEAKER_01]: I have to become a sotāpanna in order to practice dhamma.

532
00:32:26,664 --> 00:32:30,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you think you can practice dhamma without being a sotāpanna, you'll not make the effort to become a sotāpanna.

533
00:32:31,689 --> 00:32:35,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And becoming a sotāpanna is the necessary basis for beginning the practice of the dhamma.

534
00:32:47,759 --> 00:32:48,120
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

535
00:32:48,740 --> 00:32:55,730
[SPEAKER_00]: The fourth one is the taints should be understood.

536
00:32:55,790 --> 00:32:57,593
[SPEAKER_00]: The way, okay, the source and origin.

537
00:33:02,039 --> 00:33:04,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the diversity of taints?

538
00:33:06,255 --> 00:33:14,818
[SPEAKER_00]: There are taints leading to hell, leading to the animal realm, leading to the realm of ghosts, human world, or god, the god realm.

539
00:33:15,018 --> 00:33:15,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

540
00:33:15,638 --> 00:33:20,939
[SPEAKER_01]: That's sometimes when people get confused, you know, like, levels of desire, so to speak.

541
00:33:22,780 --> 00:33:25,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, now you say, acting out of desire is bad.

542
00:33:25,481 --> 00:33:26,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's true.

543
00:33:27,061 --> 00:33:30,522
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not to say that all desire is equally bad.

544
00:33:31,943 --> 00:33:35,624
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not to say that all of the taints are equally bad.

545
00:33:36,569 --> 00:33:37,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Some are worse than the other.

546
00:33:38,250 --> 00:33:39,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Some taints take you to hell.

547
00:33:40,130 --> 00:33:41,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Some taints take you to God's realms.

548
00:33:42,431 --> 00:33:43,072
[SPEAKER_01]: They're still taints.

549
00:33:44,452 --> 00:33:48,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's only like when some of these people hear, ah, so desire is bad, I must be free from desire.

550
00:33:48,854 --> 00:33:51,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, isn't practice of dhamma desire?

551
00:33:52,255 --> 00:33:52,976
[SPEAKER_01]: And so what?

552
00:33:53,096 --> 00:33:53,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it is.

553
00:33:53,716 --> 00:33:53,876
[SPEAKER_01]: And?

554
00:33:55,230 --> 00:33:55,430
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

555
00:33:55,631 --> 00:33:56,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is rooted in desire.

556
00:33:57,152 --> 00:33:59,695
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a desire that's not as bad as sensual desire.

557
00:34:00,316 --> 00:34:06,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not as bad as sensual desire, you can actually start to do things that will undermine the desire in itself.

558
00:34:07,044 --> 00:34:08,066
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll be free from desire.

559
00:34:08,726 --> 00:34:11,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you wanting to be free from desire is your own desire.

560
00:34:13,205 --> 00:34:14,065
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you do now?

561
00:34:15,046 --> 00:34:17,907
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people say, oh, isn't wanting to practice dhamma a desire?

562
00:34:18,347 --> 00:34:20,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I can't do anything about it, so I go back to sensuality.

563
00:34:21,008 --> 00:34:21,988
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there are different levels.

564
00:34:22,208 --> 00:34:25,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So why don't you then choose a better desire on account of a worse desire?

565
00:34:25,970 --> 00:34:26,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you should do.

566
00:34:27,410 --> 00:34:29,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so practicing the dhamma is a desire.

567
00:34:30,491 --> 00:34:32,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to have a sexual intercourse is a desire.

568
00:34:32,792 --> 00:34:34,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but they're not the same level desires.

569
00:34:35,553 --> 00:34:39,534
[SPEAKER_01]: So ask yourself, which desires are better than the others that I have?

570
00:34:40,195 --> 00:34:41,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And then do the better ones.

571
00:34:43,154 --> 00:34:44,995
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, find even better desires.

572
00:34:47,517 --> 00:34:48,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Start refining, exactly.

573
00:34:49,959 --> 00:34:52,480
[SPEAKER_01]: That's diversity of desire, diversity of attains.

574
00:34:52,860 --> 00:34:53,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha meant.

575
00:34:54,041 --> 00:34:56,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire is to be abandoned by the means of desire.

576
00:34:56,763 --> 00:35:00,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving.

577
00:35:00,826 --> 00:35:02,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Conceit is to be abandoned by the means of conceit.

578
00:35:03,587 --> 00:35:05,609
[SPEAKER_01]: You hear that such and such became an arahant.

579
00:35:05,729 --> 00:35:05,909
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

580
00:35:07,393 --> 00:35:08,435
[SPEAKER_01]: If he can do it, I can do it.

581
00:35:09,196 --> 00:35:14,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a completely conceited thought, because of which, now, you start looking into arahantship.

582
00:35:14,783 --> 00:35:15,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You start studying.

583
00:35:15,384 --> 00:35:17,206
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start restraining your senses.

584
00:35:17,347 --> 00:35:23,014
[SPEAKER_01]: You start dwelling on the nature of things, including the nature of your own conceit, that made you dwell on the nature of conceit.

585
00:35:23,795 --> 00:35:26,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you end up freeing yourself through your understanding of it.

586
00:35:26,297 --> 00:35:31,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, conceit resulted in cancelling the conceit by thinking in the right direction.

587
00:35:31,460 --> 00:35:37,304
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not the same conceit as someone who is so extremely arrogant.

588
00:35:37,604 --> 00:35:37,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

589
00:35:38,024 --> 00:35:39,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Who doesn't even recognize that he's not free.

590
00:35:40,026 --> 00:35:41,087
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm free from suffering.

591
00:35:41,727 --> 00:35:42,587
[SPEAKER_00]: I am ready in nibbāna.

592
00:35:42,608 --> 00:35:46,410
[SPEAKER_01]: And that means, see, knowing that means understanding the diversity of conceit.

593
00:35:46,910 --> 00:35:50,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding the diversity of desire, understanding the diversity of taints.

594
00:35:51,133 --> 00:35:51,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's different.

595
00:35:51,894 --> 00:35:52,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

596
00:35:52,634 --> 00:36:01,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, ah, okay, so, yes, this all partake in the nature of conceit, this all partakes in the nature of craving, this all partakes in the nature of desire, this all partakes in the nature of taints.

597
00:36:02,657 --> 00:36:05,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not to say that all the taints because of that are the same.

598
00:36:06,638 --> 00:36:14,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like seeing that two different contents share the same nature, and then you choose to ignore that they're different contents.

599
00:36:17,001 --> 00:36:20,342
[SPEAKER_01]: The content of this action rooted in craving

600
00:36:21,128 --> 00:36:21,889
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes you to hell.

601
00:36:21,989 --> 00:36:22,589
[SPEAKER_01]: It's murder.

602
00:36:22,949 --> 00:36:24,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean the same.

603
00:36:24,250 --> 00:36:25,912
[SPEAKER_01]: You murder somebody out of desire.

604
00:36:26,772 --> 00:36:28,413
[SPEAKER_01]: You eat food out of desire.

605
00:36:29,774 --> 00:36:32,116
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, it shares the same nature.

606
00:36:32,196 --> 00:36:33,277
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in desire.

607
00:36:33,737 --> 00:36:36,219
[SPEAKER_01]: In its content, it's a very different thing.

608
00:36:36,459 --> 00:36:38,401
[SPEAKER_01]: One is murder, another one is just eating food.

609
00:36:38,941 --> 00:36:40,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which one is better?

610
00:36:42,544 --> 00:36:43,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, do the better one.

611
00:36:44,517 --> 00:36:46,959
[SPEAKER_00]: You replace a coarser desire with a...

612
00:36:47,039 --> 00:36:47,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

613
00:36:47,279 --> 00:36:52,003
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the whole point of sensory strength and precepts, you have to replace bad action with good action.

614
00:36:52,143 --> 00:36:53,444
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still an action.

615
00:36:54,145 --> 00:37:00,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Or sometimes, then, you have people develop a belief that good action, simply replacing the bad action, is how you free yourself from action.

616
00:37:01,411 --> 00:37:02,612
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's impossible as well.

617
00:37:03,873 --> 00:37:09,538
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of action can free you from action, unless it's the action that makes you understand the nature of action.

618
00:37:09,578 --> 00:37:10,298
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll get into that.

619
00:37:10,318 --> 00:37:11,139
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll get into that.

620
00:37:11,379 --> 00:37:12,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe we'll go a bit...

621
00:37:15,502 --> 00:37:16,142
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a lot here.

622
00:37:16,462 --> 00:37:22,464
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Let's just get back on the diversity within.

623
00:37:22,484 --> 00:37:30,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like when sometimes people... You hear that you must be free from desire, and then you think all desire must stop immediately.

624
00:37:31,868 --> 00:37:33,448
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get paralyzed, you don't know what to do.

625
00:37:33,748 --> 00:37:33,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

626
00:37:34,069 --> 00:37:38,570
[SPEAKER_01]: You hear that you need to be sensory-strained, means I must stop seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting, touching things.

627
00:37:39,010 --> 00:37:39,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

628
00:37:42,672 --> 00:37:43,432
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do I do that?

629
00:37:43,672 --> 00:37:43,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Well,

630
00:37:45,003 --> 00:37:47,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Discern the diversity of the desire.

631
00:37:48,484 --> 00:37:49,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

632
00:37:49,665 --> 00:37:51,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Discern the diversity of your motivation.

633
00:37:52,386 --> 00:37:54,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, why are you doing this?

634
00:37:54,667 --> 00:38:00,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this rooted in me, me sort of, like, unwholesomely satisfying my sensuality or some practical need?

635
00:38:00,109 --> 00:38:00,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is it rooted?

636
00:38:02,189 --> 00:38:04,650
[SPEAKER_01]: So, same thing, for example, eating.

637
00:38:04,670 --> 00:38:07,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Like what the Buddha said, you should reflect upon reasons why you eat the food.

638
00:38:09,812 --> 00:38:10,132
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

639
00:38:10,152 --> 00:38:11,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same thing, same content,

640
00:38:12,655 --> 00:38:15,356
[SPEAKER_01]: But one is good action, another one is bad action.

641
00:38:15,976 --> 00:38:16,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Both are desire.

642
00:38:18,056 --> 00:38:23,217
[SPEAKER_01]: You have desire to be alive, as opposed to you have desire to have this pleasurable taste.

643
00:38:24,818 --> 00:38:29,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so desire to be alive trumps desire for pleasurable taste.

644
00:38:29,799 --> 00:38:30,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

645
00:38:30,519 --> 00:38:35,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because if I'm alive, I can get to understand the nature of desire, be free from it, and then I'll be an arahant.

646
00:38:36,417 --> 00:38:37,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it's a better design.

647
00:38:37,437 --> 00:38:38,198
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more practical.

648
00:38:38,318 --> 00:38:39,098
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a need for it.

649
00:38:39,538 --> 00:38:44,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I need this life and health to some extent so that I can practice and basically undo this life and health.

650
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:46,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Not be dependent upon it.

651
00:38:48,701 --> 00:38:52,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start discerning the diversity of your intentions, your desires, your cravings.

652
00:38:54,463 --> 00:39:03,807
[SPEAKER_01]: See, another reason why people intentionally or kind of ignorantly try to ignore the diversity of one's craving is because then

653
00:39:07,072 --> 00:39:09,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Levels it all, like, puts it on a plain field.

654
00:39:10,573 --> 00:39:15,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, see, uh, craving, in itself, has to be there.

655
00:39:15,796 --> 00:39:17,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you do, you do through craving.

656
00:39:17,956 --> 00:39:20,637
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I might as well then, you know, not be celibate.

657
00:39:21,838 --> 00:39:24,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's cravings here, cravings there, cravings anywhere.

658
00:39:24,479 --> 00:39:26,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Natural craving is in everything I want to do.

659
00:39:26,720 --> 00:39:29,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I might as well be careless and non-sense-restrained.

660
00:39:30,061 --> 00:39:31,002
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there is a difference.

661
00:39:31,442 --> 00:39:31,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

662
00:39:31,982 --> 00:39:33,383
[SPEAKER_01]: All is... all of it is craving.

663
00:39:33,483 --> 00:39:34,323
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it is desire.

664
00:39:37,002 --> 00:39:42,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, discern within that the worst content of that desire and the better content.

665
00:39:42,265 --> 00:39:43,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one will lead me out?

666
00:39:44,446 --> 00:39:46,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, which one will make this desire proliferate less?

667
00:39:48,228 --> 00:39:50,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one will make this desire pressure me less?

668
00:39:51,630 --> 00:39:57,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, when sensuality presents itself, you have desire to follow it or you can choose to not follow it.

669
00:39:57,773 --> 00:39:59,814
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can develop desire for renunciation.

670
00:40:00,695 --> 00:40:01,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said.

671
00:40:02,175 --> 00:40:05,357
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace the desire for sensuality with your renunciation.

672
00:40:08,741 --> 00:40:08,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice.

673
00:40:09,461 --> 00:40:10,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the choice.

674
00:40:10,802 --> 00:40:12,043
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no desire without choice.

675
00:40:12,063 --> 00:40:13,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Besides merit or demerit.

676
00:40:13,143 --> 00:40:14,324
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no desire without choice.

677
00:40:14,364 --> 00:40:15,344
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no choice without desire.

678
00:40:15,364 --> 00:40:16,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is better for me?

679
00:40:17,105 --> 00:40:18,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Which choice is better?

680
00:40:19,006 --> 00:40:19,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

681
00:40:19,786 --> 00:40:20,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Which choice is better?

682
00:40:21,967 --> 00:40:24,108
[SPEAKER_00]: For what my goal is?

683
00:40:24,289 --> 00:40:24,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

684
00:40:24,729 --> 00:40:29,311
[SPEAKER_01]: The clearer you are about your values and your goal, the clearer you'll be about choices and your responsibilities.

685
00:40:29,911 --> 00:40:31,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to worry about it's all desire.

686
00:40:31,732 --> 00:40:32,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do then?

687
00:40:33,273 --> 00:40:33,933
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a question.

688
00:40:33,973 --> 00:40:34,614
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do then?

689
00:40:34,654 --> 00:40:35,994
[SPEAKER_01]: It's another desire.

690
00:40:36,034 --> 00:40:37,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire to figure this out.

691
00:40:39,467 --> 00:40:45,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you do then is, don't... stop making things worse through your careless choices and careless desires.

692
00:40:46,109 --> 00:40:47,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Create careless acts out of your desires.

693
00:40:55,091 --> 00:40:59,212
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cessation of taints?

694
00:40:59,252 --> 00:41:01,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of ignorance.

695
00:41:01,792 --> 00:41:02,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of taints.

696
00:41:03,993 --> 00:41:05,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding those things.

697
00:41:06,423 --> 00:41:07,704
[SPEAKER_01]: The only one.

698
00:41:07,724 --> 00:41:12,648
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not having a special experience, you know, or I had experience of cessation.

699
00:41:13,248 --> 00:41:14,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but what does that mean?

700
00:41:14,169 --> 00:41:16,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you understood it?

701
00:41:16,170 --> 00:41:20,373
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you have experience of cessation if it's not understood?

702
00:41:20,393 --> 00:41:21,834
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's not a cessation.

703
00:41:23,876 --> 00:41:26,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what I said, cessation is understanding of those things.

704
00:41:27,618 --> 00:41:28,439
[SPEAKER_01]: So, have you understood it?

705
00:41:28,879 --> 00:41:31,021
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, convey it to me, please.

706
00:41:31,101 --> 00:41:33,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding is the diminishing of ignorance.

707
00:41:33,923 --> 00:41:35,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which is what cessation is.

708
00:41:37,078 --> 00:41:41,601
[SPEAKER_01]: But more often than not, people regard, oh, cessation is this experience when everything has ceased.

709
00:41:42,422 --> 00:41:45,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And now I need to understand that and find my way of interpreting it.

710
00:41:46,345 --> 00:41:47,606
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you haven't understood it.

711
00:41:48,787 --> 00:41:53,571
[SPEAKER_01]: It means your experience of cessation has arisen for you.

712
00:41:54,491 --> 00:41:55,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Has nothing ceased.

713
00:41:56,473 --> 00:41:57,334
[SPEAKER_01]: You just added more.

714
00:41:59,215 --> 00:41:59,936
[SPEAKER_00]: That's quite funny.

715
00:42:00,636 --> 00:42:03,398
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a contradiction in terms.

716
00:42:03,498 --> 00:42:04,139
[SPEAKER_00]: A special...

717
00:42:05,843 --> 00:42:12,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Weird experience that I had during my intense meditation is the ending of ignorance.

718
00:42:13,125 --> 00:42:13,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

719
00:42:13,805 --> 00:42:15,245
[SPEAKER_01]: I had, like, when people...

720
00:42:15,265 --> 00:42:16,946
[SPEAKER_01]: I have experience when everything is ceased.

721
00:42:17,346 --> 00:42:18,706
[SPEAKER_01]: I had experience of nothingness.

722
00:42:19,946 --> 00:42:21,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you talk about it?

723
00:42:21,787 --> 00:42:23,807
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you designating if there is nothing there?

724
00:42:25,107 --> 00:42:27,368
[SPEAKER_01]: If there was nothing there, what are you aware of?

725
00:42:29,202 --> 00:42:30,743
[SPEAKER_01]: There has to be something for you to be aware of it.

726
00:42:31,824 --> 00:42:35,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, when you say cessation, when you say it's nothing, it's actually very much something.

727
00:42:37,328 --> 00:42:38,208
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no, no, no.

728
00:42:38,429 --> 00:42:39,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing is ceased.

729
00:42:39,469 --> 00:42:44,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, nothing that you kind of commonly know was there.

730
00:42:45,220 --> 00:42:48,283
[SPEAKER_01]: That means an uncommon, a novelty experience was replaced.

731
00:42:48,383 --> 00:42:50,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance has ceased in regard to that thing.

732
00:42:50,545 --> 00:42:59,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the Buddha said, when he talks about nothingness and cessation, it's always cessation of these things that determine your experience of something or anything.

733
00:43:00,094 --> 00:43:07,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's cessation of ignorance, cessation of lust, cessation of aversion, cessation of delusion, not the experience of cessation.

734
00:43:08,513 --> 00:43:14,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Which you're very much lustful for, or averse if somebody attacks it, or ignore it if he starts making you doubt it.

735
00:43:15,896 --> 00:43:22,079
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the experience of cessation, it's cessation of greed, aversion, illusion, in regard to any experience as such.

736
00:43:22,639 --> 00:43:23,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

737
00:43:32,223 --> 00:43:34,244
[SPEAKER_00]: And, sorry, number five only.

738
00:43:35,672 --> 00:43:37,253
[SPEAKER_00]: What is karma?

739
00:43:37,733 --> 00:43:40,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Action should be understood.

740
00:43:41,294 --> 00:43:42,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice should be understood.

741
00:43:42,995 --> 00:43:47,237
[SPEAKER_00]: It is volition, bhikkhus, that I call.

742
00:43:48,886 --> 00:43:52,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention is action.

743
00:43:52,689 --> 00:43:57,212
[SPEAKER_00]: For having intended one acts by body, speech or mind.

744
00:43:57,232 --> 00:43:59,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Cetanā, strictly speaking, is intentional intention.

745
00:44:00,915 --> 00:44:02,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So, choice.

746
00:44:02,677 --> 00:44:06,740
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when you're making a choice, you have all these possible ways of intending.

747
00:44:07,361 --> 00:44:10,263
[SPEAKER_01]: I can intend it for this, or for this, or for this, or for this.

748
00:44:10,443 --> 00:44:10,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

749
00:44:11,704 --> 00:44:14,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I choose to intend this given intention.

750
00:44:15,504 --> 00:44:16,245
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's your choice.

751
00:44:16,385 --> 00:44:19,928
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to make a choice on account of present possibilities.

752
00:44:20,208 --> 00:44:21,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Each possibility is an intention.

753
00:44:22,710 --> 00:44:25,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally choosing that intention is what cetanā is.

754
00:44:25,492 --> 00:44:26,533
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already kāma.

755
00:44:26,553 --> 00:44:27,974
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already where your kāma is rooted.

756
00:44:29,075 --> 00:44:33,439
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the origin of action, contact?

757
00:44:33,999 --> 00:44:35,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure.

758
00:44:35,420 --> 00:44:36,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is the origin of your choice.

759
00:44:37,502 --> 00:44:38,063
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

760
00:44:38,503 --> 00:44:39,384
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose

761
00:44:41,065 --> 00:44:44,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of the value of I must be free from the discomfort of the pressure.

762
00:44:44,466 --> 00:44:47,848
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a gratuitous value that's maintained through ignoring it.

763
00:44:48,348 --> 00:44:53,810
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why whatever your choice should you make, you make it on account of how you feel and trying to get rid of whatever you feel.

764
00:44:54,791 --> 00:44:58,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Get rid of the pleasurable feeling by wanting to replace it with more of that pleasure.

765
00:44:59,693 --> 00:45:04,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Get rid of the unpleasurable feeling by wanting to replace it but less unpleasurable.

766
00:45:04,988 --> 00:45:12,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And getting rid of the neutral feeling by replacing it... getting rid of the discomfort of the neutral feeling by replacing it with comfortable feeling, whatever that is.

767
00:45:13,811 --> 00:45:14,351
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

768
00:45:15,031 --> 00:45:18,192
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a choice to not choose to act out of pressure.

769
00:45:20,853 --> 00:45:21,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is there.

770
00:45:21,914 --> 00:45:22,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

771
00:45:23,814 --> 00:45:27,095
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, there is no first point when you're not pressured, then you become pressured.

772
00:45:27,636 --> 00:45:29,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You wake up and you find yourself pressured.

773
00:45:31,497 --> 00:45:32,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your point of view.

774
00:45:33,017 --> 00:45:33,698
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your world.

775
00:45:33,718 --> 00:45:34,598
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your universe.

776
00:45:35,875 --> 00:45:36,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You can...

777
00:45:36,415 --> 00:45:39,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have a choice to stop acting out of that pressure.

778
00:45:40,278 --> 00:45:44,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, what you said, there is a diversity of action.

779
00:45:44,320 --> 00:45:44,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, diversity of choices.

780
00:45:44,981 --> 00:45:46,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Diversity of what's acting at you.

781
00:45:46,642 --> 00:45:47,062
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant.

782
00:45:47,082 --> 00:45:50,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sensuality presents to you, you have a choice to say no.

783
00:45:50,924 --> 00:45:51,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

784
00:45:51,645 --> 00:45:54,507
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a choice to be non-reactive to the pressure.

785
00:45:54,527 --> 00:45:55,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's a choice.

786
00:45:55,247 --> 00:45:57,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's still an action, a choice.

787
00:45:57,468 --> 00:46:02,912
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that you're much more pressured by other choices and you choose to choose out of the pressure.

788
00:46:04,317 --> 00:46:09,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you keep maintaining the pressure.

789
00:46:09,439 --> 00:46:11,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Complete, like, gratuitous reason that you choose it.

790
00:46:13,820 --> 00:46:15,221
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose it because you don't understand it.

791
00:46:15,521 --> 00:46:18,102
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to understand it, you would realize it's impossible actually to choose it.

792
00:46:19,322 --> 00:46:21,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not a good idea to be choosing it.

793
00:46:21,563 --> 00:46:24,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that might... that understanding might come before.

794
00:46:25,684 --> 00:46:29,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you understand it's impossible to choose it, then it ceases to even be a possibility as an idea.

795
00:46:30,506 --> 00:46:31,306
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's impossible.

796
00:46:36,222 --> 00:46:40,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure was always secondary to the experience as a whole.

797
00:46:40,726 --> 00:46:43,288
[SPEAKER_01]: That was not changed even when I thought pressure was first.

798
00:46:43,728 --> 00:46:46,230
[SPEAKER_01]: In my experience, my choices, my feelings are second.

799
00:46:47,111 --> 00:46:49,153
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it was... that was just my perception of it.

800
00:46:49,553 --> 00:46:50,894
[SPEAKER_01]: The only perception that I had.

801
00:46:51,855 --> 00:46:53,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you have done it, that's why it's irreversible.

802
00:46:53,997 --> 00:46:56,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Process of arahantship or getting the right view.

803
00:46:56,359 --> 00:46:57,500
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't reverse it back.

804
00:46:57,640 --> 00:46:58,401
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't forget it.

805
00:46:59,021 --> 00:47:02,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it pertains to the nature of your experience that now you understood.

806
00:47:02,952 --> 00:47:04,253
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you can't ignore it anymore.

807
00:47:04,313 --> 00:47:04,854
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

808
00:47:04,874 --> 00:47:07,396
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you were ignoring it is because you were not understanding it.

809
00:47:07,416 --> 00:47:08,577
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't twist it out of shape.

810
00:47:08,718 --> 00:47:09,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

811
00:47:09,758 --> 00:47:11,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally.

812
00:47:11,220 --> 00:47:12,081
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

813
00:47:12,121 --> 00:47:15,223
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be twisted out of shape.

814
00:47:15,324 --> 00:47:16,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally or unintentionally.

815
00:47:22,536 --> 00:47:25,277
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cessation of action?

816
00:47:25,317 --> 00:47:26,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of contact.

817
00:47:26,377 --> 00:47:27,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of pressure.

818
00:47:27,878 --> 00:47:28,998
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the cessation of pressure?

819
00:47:29,099 --> 00:47:30,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not acting out of pressure.

820
00:47:30,499 --> 00:47:32,240
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the vicious circle I was talking about.

821
00:47:32,720 --> 00:47:33,880
[SPEAKER_01]: See, out of pressure, you act.

822
00:47:34,240 --> 00:47:35,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that action, you're pressured.

823
00:47:36,021 --> 00:47:36,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of pressure, you act.

824
00:47:36,982 --> 00:47:37,902
[SPEAKER_01]: The fuel is the pressure.

825
00:47:37,942 --> 00:47:38,642
[SPEAKER_01]: The fuel is the action.

826
00:47:38,662 --> 00:47:39,362
[SPEAKER_01]: The fuel is the pressure.

827
00:47:39,382 --> 00:47:40,023
[SPEAKER_01]: The fuel is the action.

828
00:47:40,043 --> 00:47:41,143
[SPEAKER_00]: The fuel is the action.

829
00:47:41,163 --> 00:47:42,544
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a vicious circle.

830
00:47:42,564 --> 00:47:44,885
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start choosing not acting out of pressure.

831
00:47:45,225 --> 00:47:46,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, sense restraint.

832
00:47:46,505 --> 00:47:48,526
[SPEAKER_00]: The merging of that... Yeah.

833
00:47:49,722 --> 00:47:51,884
[SPEAKER_01]: And it ties in well with what we said before.

834
00:47:51,904 --> 00:47:53,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like I must say no to everything.

835
00:47:54,125 --> 00:47:54,285
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

836
00:47:54,946 --> 00:47:57,188
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to say no to acting when you're pressured.

837
00:47:58,448 --> 00:48:01,911
[SPEAKER_01]: When you feel emotional need to act, that's when you say no to yourself.

838
00:48:04,273 --> 00:48:05,394
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't feel emotional need to eat.

839
00:48:05,434 --> 00:48:06,294
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm simply hungry.

840
00:48:07,255 --> 00:48:09,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll eat for hunger.

841
00:48:10,437 --> 00:48:14,020
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you're learning how to act not rooted in pressure.

842
00:48:16,623 --> 00:48:23,086
[SPEAKER_01]: By refining your action, by making it less rooted in pressure, the coarser actions out of pressure will fall off.

843
00:48:23,566 --> 00:48:30,508
[SPEAKER_01]: By refining your action further, making it even less, less and less rooted in pressure, what was coarser to that will fall off.

844
00:48:31,448 --> 00:48:37,430
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, it's not a blanket method that you can just say no to everything, because not everything equally pressures you.

845
00:48:37,470 --> 00:48:37,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

846
00:48:37,971 --> 00:48:39,471
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not understanding it.

847
00:48:39,491 --> 00:48:40,471
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what we always talk about.

848
00:48:40,492 --> 00:48:44,433
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know where your motivation is rooted, because you are motivated by the pressure.

849
00:48:45,352 --> 00:48:53,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by finding out why you're doing something you want to do, or defending something you want to defend, or want to gain something you want to gain, you will find out whether you're pressured by it or not.

850
00:48:55,138 --> 00:48:57,700
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're pressured, you say no to acting out of pressure.

851
00:49:04,727 --> 00:49:08,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why certain things must be abandoned, because they cannot not be rooted in pressure.

852
00:49:09,551 --> 00:49:18,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Sexual intercourse, killing, stealing, cheating, lying, always, action in itself, cannot be done in any other way except rooted in pressure.

853
00:49:19,658 --> 00:49:23,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, oh, you can have sexual intercourse, not being pressured by sensuality.

854
00:49:24,381 --> 00:49:25,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Andy would have said that, actually.

855
00:49:25,942 --> 00:49:30,185
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to engage in sensuality without having perceptions of sensuality.

856
00:49:31,832 --> 00:49:33,913
[SPEAKER_01]: And perceptions of sensuality are determined by what?

857
00:49:34,153 --> 00:49:38,075
[SPEAKER_01]: By the pressure of sensuality, as perceptions are determined by pressure in general.

858
00:49:38,195 --> 00:49:44,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, one begins trying to understand, trying to make things clear, trying to understand what's going on.

859
00:49:44,899 --> 00:49:51,142
[SPEAKER_01]: You begin that by looking where your motivation for action and choices is, whatever the action and choices are.

860
00:49:51,523 --> 00:49:54,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, now I'm doing it, doing it, doing it.

861
00:49:54,524 --> 00:49:56,005
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm getting better at it.

862
00:49:56,705 --> 00:49:58,626
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't... you don't even have to

863
00:50:01,647 --> 00:50:05,769
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't question it that much, if you think... No, because you're knowing where you look, you know where to look.

864
00:50:05,789 --> 00:50:06,529
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what this thing is.

865
00:50:06,549 --> 00:50:07,150
[SPEAKER_00]: You know where to look.

866
00:50:07,170 --> 00:50:07,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

867
00:50:07,690 --> 00:50:07,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

868
00:50:08,170 --> 00:50:08,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

869
00:50:08,790 --> 00:50:14,173
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm so familiar with this thing, that when it comes up, when the pressure's there, I know it already.

870
00:50:14,193 --> 00:50:14,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

871
00:50:14,833 --> 00:50:17,734
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to go and look at the suit.

872
00:50:17,775 --> 00:50:21,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Inevitably, when people start questioning things, it's going to be on the level of psychologizing.

873
00:50:21,596 --> 00:50:22,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, why is this?

874
00:50:22,657 --> 00:50:23,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

875
00:50:23,217 --> 00:50:26,358
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be like, you know, an arm's length away

876
00:50:30,169 --> 00:50:31,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't take that as face value.

877
00:50:31,550 --> 00:50:33,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Start questioning your questioning, so to speak.

878
00:50:33,752 --> 00:50:34,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so why do I want to do that?

879
00:50:34,993 --> 00:50:38,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's because yesterday such and such person said this, so today I feel...

880
00:50:38,875 --> 00:50:41,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so can I be more immediate, more accurate?

881
00:50:42,098 --> 00:50:45,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I find a more here-and-now reason for why I want to do this here and now?

882
00:50:46,961 --> 00:50:49,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, it's because of something yesterday.

883
00:50:49,323 --> 00:50:51,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you choosing that to be the reason for acting.

884
00:50:52,905 --> 00:50:57,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means even that choice now is rooted by something more immediate you're not seeing.

885
00:50:57,689 --> 00:50:58,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So why did you choose?

886
00:50:59,697 --> 00:51:03,819
[SPEAKER_01]: That event that happened yesterday to be the reason for your action tomorrow.

887
00:51:05,940 --> 00:51:12,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that event that happened yesterday displeased me, made me feel uncomfortable.

888
00:51:13,783 --> 00:51:16,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to address it tomorrow.

889
00:51:16,284 --> 00:51:16,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

890
00:51:16,764 --> 00:51:20,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, it's the discomfort that's the problem, not what happened yesterday.

891
00:51:21,586 --> 00:51:26,268
[SPEAKER_01]: If that which happened yesterday, if they were not uncomfortable, would you be planning dealing with it tomorrow?

892
00:51:26,918 --> 00:51:27,058
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

893
00:51:27,719 --> 00:51:31,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's that more immediate reason for you wanting to deal with tomorrow?

894
00:51:31,161 --> 00:51:35,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, I want to deal, manage, prevent further discomfort.

895
00:51:37,045 --> 00:51:38,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Which I'm liable to.

896
00:51:39,007 --> 00:51:39,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

897
00:51:39,407 --> 00:51:40,048
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but that's the thing.

898
00:51:40,608 --> 00:51:42,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you want to prevent discomfort?

899
00:51:42,369 --> 00:51:43,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm liable to it.

900
00:51:44,471 --> 00:51:49,094
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you prevent your liability to it by addressing the individual discomfort that already happened?

901
00:51:49,895 --> 00:51:50,055
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

902
00:51:50,835 --> 00:51:51,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You remain equally liable.

903
00:51:53,188 --> 00:51:54,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Some other discomfort is going to arise.

904
00:51:55,149 --> 00:52:02,512
[SPEAKER_01]: And ask yourself, how many times in the past you tried to manage discomfort by addressing this issue, that issue, or controlling, or psychologizing about it, figuring it out?

905
00:52:03,933 --> 00:52:05,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Has that diminished your reliability?

906
00:52:06,174 --> 00:52:06,294
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

907
00:52:06,454 --> 00:52:09,535
[SPEAKER_01]: That just increased your skill of managing it.

908
00:52:10,176 --> 00:52:11,796
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're still equally affected by it.

909
00:52:13,357 --> 00:52:17,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if you were to learn how to not be affected by it in the first place?

910
00:52:17,719 --> 00:52:18,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Would there be anything for you to manage?

911
00:52:19,560 --> 00:52:19,680
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

912
00:52:20,280 --> 00:52:21,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why can't I learn

913
00:52:22,483 --> 00:52:23,524
[SPEAKER_01]: To accept the discomfort.

914
00:52:24,584 --> 00:52:28,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I need to deal with it, get rid of it, act out of it?

915
00:52:28,287 --> 00:52:32,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I need, why am I pressured to choose action that wants to get rid of the discomfort?

916
00:52:34,311 --> 00:52:40,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, because I never made the opposite effort, of not acting out of discomfort.

917
00:52:42,925 --> 00:52:55,349
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the cessation of contact is the cessation of, kind of, that habit of... Cessation of pressure is basically achieved through not acting out of pressure that maintains the pressure.

918
00:52:55,369 --> 00:52:56,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which you've been doing.

919
00:52:56,509 --> 00:52:58,250
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then bring the understanding of it.

920
00:52:58,630 --> 00:52:58,810
Yeah.

921
00:53:01,571 --> 00:53:09,593
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean, like, the dhamma can only be found within sīla, within sensory strain, within non-sensuality.

922
00:53:12,118 --> 00:53:15,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like after, or later on, a second step.

923
00:53:16,345 --> 00:53:17,006
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within it.

924
00:53:17,647 --> 00:53:22,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by being sense-restrained and celibate and so on, you are emulating the dhamma.

925
00:53:24,157 --> 00:53:25,099
[SPEAKER_01]: By emulating it,

926
00:53:25,953 --> 00:53:28,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually have a chance of understanding what you're creating.

927
00:53:28,836 --> 00:53:29,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

928
00:53:29,537 --> 00:53:33,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, emulating in itself, if you don't make the effort to understand, it will not result in understanding.

929
00:53:34,181 --> 00:53:34,962
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still wholesome.

930
00:53:35,803 --> 00:53:41,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still wholesome to not give in to the worst type of the diversification of your desire.

931
00:53:42,189 --> 00:53:48,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the one thing is that I do it for a week, central strength, and then I go back to six.

932
00:53:49,207 --> 00:53:54,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which means you never addressed the ultimate value of sexual intercourse or sensuality.

933
00:53:55,052 --> 00:53:57,914
[SPEAKER_01]: You only try to ignore it for a week by being sensory-strained.

934
00:53:58,794 --> 00:53:59,615
[SPEAKER_00]: That is not...

935
00:53:59,635 --> 00:54:00,796
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to be other way around.

936
00:54:01,176 --> 00:54:06,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to value sensory-strained, celibacy and non-sexual activity forever.

937
00:54:06,901 --> 00:54:10,724
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you do engage with it, it will be obvious it's because of weakness.

938
00:54:11,718 --> 00:54:13,599
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you will be able to uproot it.

939
00:54:14,079 --> 00:54:23,582
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never undermine the value of sexual intercourse essentiality, and only occasionally abstain yourself from it, means, oh, that's the wrong way around.

940
00:54:24,523 --> 00:54:28,884
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to undermine the value, even if you don't occasionally abstain from it.

941
00:54:29,605 --> 00:54:29,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

942
00:54:29,825 --> 00:54:38,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by undermining value, you will occasionally, and then much more profoundly, and maybe even for the rest of your life, abstain from it.

943
00:54:40,143 --> 00:54:43,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you now undermine the view that sensuality is value.

944
00:54:43,245 --> 00:54:47,669
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we would teach people not that... We would teach them directly that sensuality is dangerous.

945
00:54:48,229 --> 00:54:51,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the value that you have in regard to sensual pleasure is wrong.

946
00:54:52,332 --> 00:54:54,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you value that which is deadly.

947
00:54:54,955 --> 00:54:57,156
[SPEAKER_01]: You value that which is harmful.

948
00:54:57,176 --> 00:54:59,638
[SPEAKER_01]: You value that which is factually painful.

949
00:55:00,619 --> 00:55:01,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And then people say, thank you very much.

950
00:55:02,300 --> 00:55:04,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Get the right view and go back to that sensuality.

951
00:55:05,543 --> 00:55:06,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Acts of that sensuality.

952
00:55:06,604 --> 00:55:07,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sotāpānas, for example.

953
00:55:08,735 --> 00:55:12,527
[SPEAKER_01]: But they will never be able to get rid of now.

954
00:55:13,712 --> 00:55:16,092
[SPEAKER_01]: The view that sensuality is bad.

955
00:55:16,713 --> 00:55:29,635
[SPEAKER_01]: They will be too weak, initially, if they choose to not make the effort, if they're satisfied with understanding that sensuality is really painful, but they will be too weak to maintain that standard until they're fully freed from any sensual itch.

956
00:55:30,475 --> 00:55:32,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they might still scratch their itch, but they will know what they're doing.

957
00:55:33,295 --> 00:55:35,176
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot say, oh, no, sensuality is not a problem.

958
00:55:35,656 --> 00:55:41,817
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that is like the pernicious view, as the Buddha said in another sutra, that is when they say, there is no danger in sensuality.

959
00:55:41,877 --> 00:55:42,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like...

960
00:55:43,037 --> 00:55:46,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's... he's restrained or he's not practicing sense restraint.

961
00:55:47,438 --> 00:55:53,580
[SPEAKER_01]: That's secondary to whether you think sensuality is dangerous or not really that dangerous.

962
00:55:54,800 --> 00:56:04,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that... actually, that one, it was a monk, who was obviously celibate, sense restrained, but still had the view that sensuality is not dangerous.

963
00:56:05,422 --> 00:56:10,105
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha called him amidst the assembly and absolutely hammered him.

964
00:56:10,125 --> 00:56:17,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, called him names and stuff for holding that ridiculous view because of which charnel grounds gets filled and so on.

965
00:56:18,091 --> 00:56:26,938
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would have been less wrong for him to lose his sense restraint but not adopt the view that sensuality is not dangerous.

966
00:56:27,977 --> 00:56:34,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you have a view that sensuality is dangerous, there is only so far you can lose your sense restraint.

967
00:56:34,223 --> 00:56:39,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, you know, going down into this pit will kill me in the most horrifying death.

968
00:56:39,909 --> 00:56:43,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can maybe carelessly walk in two or three steps, and then you'll be out.

969
00:56:43,813 --> 00:56:45,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the danger will be way...

970
00:56:45,795 --> 00:57:01,922
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be apparent, not like fundamentally apparent, so you'd never even stop your foot in the cave, step your foot in the cave, but it's certainly apparent enough for you to, like, do things of that diversified nature that would take you in the hell, rooted in those type of taints.

971
00:57:04,983 --> 00:57:05,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

972
00:57:05,623 --> 00:57:08,104
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that view that you want to undermine.

973
00:57:09,485 --> 00:57:14,547
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will never undermine that view if you don't practice sense restraint at all,

974
00:57:16,603 --> 00:57:17,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Even to begin with.

975
00:57:18,944 --> 00:57:24,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, ah, generosity, virtue, the mind were ready, we would say.

976
00:57:24,166 --> 00:57:29,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, he would teach them the danger of sensuality, that now they were withdrawn from, through listening to his talk.

977
00:57:30,548 --> 00:57:33,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And now the dangers... and then he would teach them dhamma, and they would get the right view.

978
00:57:37,111 --> 00:57:41,873
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if they go back to sensuality, to their whole, like, lay life, they will never ever

979
00:57:42,824 --> 00:57:46,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Think of sensuality as non-dangerous, or as fine, or as not a big deal.

980
00:57:47,566 --> 00:58:00,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they know for the rest of their life, they will be acting out of weakness, they're living with family, still engaging in... they're still always giving the five precepts, sometimes eight precepts, and so on, but finally they know they're not making effort to uproot the danger.

981
00:58:01,472 --> 00:58:03,253
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot have the view that it's not dangerous.

982
00:58:03,273 --> 00:58:03,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would...

983
00:58:04,354 --> 00:58:09,876
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's ridiculous to think that... that such a person would...

984
00:58:11,660 --> 00:58:12,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Promote sensuality.

985
00:58:13,041 --> 00:58:13,221
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

986
00:58:13,582 --> 00:58:16,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I see the danger, but I'll promote it for others.

987
00:58:16,304 --> 00:58:17,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible, impossible.

988
00:58:17,024 --> 00:58:17,665
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll promote it.

989
00:58:18,145 --> 00:58:21,347
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I'm engaging with sensuality because I'm still weak.

990
00:58:21,848 --> 00:58:23,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I will defend the danger.

991
00:58:23,069 --> 00:58:30,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not making effort to uproot my weakness because, well, I have family, children, wives and so on, whatever else, back in the day.

992
00:58:31,355 --> 00:58:38,621
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll just have to accept responsibility for my weakness and be satisfied with the right view, the Lord that taught me that the danger of sensuality, freedom from suffering and so on.

993
00:58:39,518 --> 00:58:50,847
[SPEAKER_01]: But not under any circumstance will I say, oh, it's fine to say that sensuality is not bad, because that's far worse than my individual weakness of engaging with sensuality.

994
00:58:51,488 --> 00:58:56,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Or saying, oh, you shouldn't say that sensuality is dangerous because it might upset people.

995
00:58:56,812 --> 00:58:56,973
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

996
00:58:57,753 --> 00:58:59,475
[SPEAKER_01]: You will never ever compromise

997
00:59:00,121 --> 00:59:04,164
[SPEAKER_01]: On the view, if you have the right view, because you realize, well, if you compromise it, it's not right anymore.

998
00:59:04,744 --> 00:59:07,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no like, oh, well, this works, so this doesn't know.

999
00:59:07,546 --> 00:59:12,089
[SPEAKER_01]: The views are the right, where you know what dangerous is dangerous and non-dangerous is non-dangerous.

1000
00:59:12,250 --> 00:59:14,971
[SPEAKER_01]: One with right view doesn't... cannot conceal.

1001
00:59:15,912 --> 00:59:17,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

1002
00:59:17,414 --> 00:59:17,994
[SPEAKER_01]: For what reason?

1003
00:59:18,295 --> 00:59:20,837
[SPEAKER_01]: And the truth is, oh, I'm weak, I'm not free from sensuality.

1004
00:59:21,138 --> 00:59:23,580
[SPEAKER_01]: The truth is not, oh, sensuality is not that bad.

1005
00:59:24,241 --> 00:59:24,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1006
00:59:25,342 --> 00:59:26,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

1007
00:59:26,023 --> 00:59:32,650
[SPEAKER_01]: The person who uttered that, that the Buddha destroyed in the assembly, was a monk himself who was restrained living by the patimokkha.

1008
00:59:33,432 --> 00:59:36,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on the surface, he was more restrained than your average person.

1009
00:59:37,173 --> 00:59:38,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Far more restrained.

1010
00:59:38,973 --> 00:59:41,454
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not why the Buddha criticized him or praised him.

1011
00:59:41,774 --> 00:59:45,655
[SPEAKER_01]: It was because he held the view that there is no danger in sensuality.

1012
00:59:46,255 --> 00:59:51,997
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm restrained and I will be restrained for the rest of my life, but I don't see this danger in sensuality that the Lord talks about.

1013
00:59:52,037 --> 00:59:52,957
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no obstruction.

1014
00:59:53,197 --> 00:59:55,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is not obstruction.

1015
00:59:55,137 --> 00:59:55,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

1016
00:59:55,478 --> 00:59:56,278
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no danger in it.

1017
00:59:56,458 --> 00:59:56,838
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fine.

1018
00:59:57,418 --> 00:59:58,438
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do it or not do it.

1019
00:59:58,538 --> 00:59:58,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Up to you.

1020
00:59:58,958 --> 00:59:59,739
[SPEAKER_01]: However you feel like it.

1021
00:59:59,859 --> 01:00:00,599
[SPEAKER_01]: I choose to do it.

1022
01:00:01,219 --> 01:00:01,979
[SPEAKER_01]: to be restrained.

1023
01:00:02,580 --> 01:00:04,801
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, there is no obstruction in it.

1024
01:00:04,821 --> 01:00:07,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, that's... that's the core of the problem.

1025
01:00:07,682 --> 01:00:09,023
[SPEAKER_00]: There's even a rule, isn't it?

1026
01:00:09,043 --> 01:00:09,643
[SPEAKER_00]: There's even a rule, yeah.

1027
01:00:09,663 --> 01:00:11,124
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll get kicked out, basically, eventually.

1028
01:00:11,184 --> 01:00:11,964
[SPEAKER_01]: As a novice, yeah.

1029
01:00:12,124 --> 01:00:15,345
[SPEAKER_01]: You just... if you... Actually, even as a monk, you'll be kicked out of the community.

1030
01:00:15,405 --> 01:00:16,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1031
01:00:16,066 --> 01:00:18,567
[SPEAKER_01]: If you hold these views, that there is no danger in sensuality.

1032
01:00:20,008 --> 01:00:25,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, okay, so that... and that would also... you could... if people suddenly struggle and say, so how would Svatantipānda go back to sensuality?

1033
01:00:25,529 --> 01:00:25,989
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how.

1034
01:00:26,610 --> 01:00:28,050
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows he's done out of weakness.

1035
01:00:28,570 --> 01:00:31,731
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows he's responsible for not making effort to uproot that weakness.

1036
01:00:31,791 --> 01:00:37,472
[SPEAKER_01]: But what he cannot do is regard sensuality as not dangerous or as non-obstructive.

1037
01:00:37,592 --> 01:00:40,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever he were to pick up his practice again,

1038
01:00:41,053 --> 01:00:43,415
[SPEAKER_01]: He will do so by abandoning sensuality.

1039
01:00:43,955 --> 01:00:54,341
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why nekama-saṅgapa, pretty much, thoughts of renunciation, picking up sense restraint, is all that's necessary for a person with the right view to develop that right view to arahant-ji.

1040
01:00:56,663 --> 01:01:00,505
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's asked, is sensuality good?

1041
01:01:00,985 --> 01:01:02,326
[SPEAKER_01]: He would say, not a chance.

1042
01:01:02,346 --> 01:01:04,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you say, well, why are you still then living with your family?

1043
01:01:04,728 --> 01:01:05,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm weak.

1044
01:01:06,769 --> 01:01:07,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah.

1045
01:01:07,789 --> 01:01:08,850
[SPEAKER_01]: It's end of the argument.

1046
01:01:09,030 --> 01:01:09,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1047
01:01:09,991 --> 01:01:10,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

1048
01:01:12,406 --> 01:01:20,470
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm not as weak as a person who is weak and chooses to mask their weakness as strength by saying, there is no danger in spirituality.

1049
01:01:22,371 --> 01:01:23,231
[SPEAKER_01]: That's foulness.

1050
01:01:24,832 --> 01:01:28,794
[SPEAKER_00]: And just to get to the last point, if we can...

1051
01:01:32,176 --> 01:01:33,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering should be understood.

1052
01:01:34,938 --> 01:01:36,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I was going to say that in the beginning.

1053
01:01:36,178 --> 01:01:38,780
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why this whole principle is the principle of the Four Noble Truths.

1054
01:01:40,114 --> 01:01:41,714
[SPEAKER_00]: The source and origin should be understood.

1055
01:01:41,734 --> 01:01:42,355
[SPEAKER_01]: What is suffering?

1056
01:01:42,395 --> 01:01:43,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is suffering.

1057
01:01:43,295 --> 01:01:45,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1058
01:01:45,756 --> 01:01:46,876
[SPEAKER_00]: The diversity.

1059
01:01:47,916 --> 01:01:48,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1060
01:01:48,616 --> 01:01:49,737
[SPEAKER_00]: There's extreme suffering.

1061
01:01:49,797 --> 01:01:50,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

1062
01:01:50,037 --> 01:01:50,817
[SPEAKER_00]: There's slight suffering.

1063
01:01:50,837 --> 01:01:52,137
[SPEAKER_01]: There is beneficial suffering.

1064
01:01:53,198 --> 01:01:54,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering that fades slowly.

1065
01:01:54,578 --> 01:01:55,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering that fades quickly.

1066
01:01:55,858 --> 01:01:58,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering of the sensory strength is beneficial suffering.

1067
01:01:59,519 --> 01:02:04,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering on account of not getting enough of what you want is not beneficial suffering.

1068
01:02:04,941 --> 01:02:05,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

1069
01:02:05,221 --> 01:02:08,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering on account of saying no to your desire is a beneficial suffering.

1070
01:02:09,018 --> 01:02:18,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it creates a possibility for you to now understand the nature of acting out of non-desire, or acting out of saying no to desire.

1071
01:02:19,820 --> 01:02:24,781
[SPEAKER_01]: And that possibility will not exist for those who don't have even basic sense restraint.

1072
01:02:37,843 --> 01:02:38,763
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cessation?

1073
01:02:40,890 --> 01:02:47,734
[SPEAKER_00]: suffering, the cessation of craving, which is it, which is that very thing that fuels the contact, the pressure.

1074
01:02:47,874 --> 01:02:48,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1075
01:02:50,716 --> 01:02:59,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And, can you say that the sotāpanna

1076
01:03:01,365 --> 01:03:17,217
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, that's very much what frees you.

1077
01:03:17,718 --> 01:03:26,925
[SPEAKER_01]: See, like, imagine you're trapped in the house, and the roof is basically eroding, falling down on you, bit by bit, and you don't know how to escape.

1078
01:03:27,765 --> 01:03:29,547
[SPEAKER_01]: The real suffering there is that fear.

1079
01:03:30,728 --> 01:03:33,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Of not being able to escape what will happen.

1080
01:03:34,849 --> 01:03:37,710
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the despair, the terror.

1081
01:03:38,571 --> 01:03:39,991
[SPEAKER_01]: But then imagine you figure out a way out.

1082
01:03:40,792 --> 01:03:42,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you look at the roof and say, ah, I still have time.

1083
01:03:42,972 --> 01:03:43,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you do, in a way.

1084
01:03:45,113 --> 01:03:47,754
[SPEAKER_01]: But, of course, from Arhat's point of view, even that's careless.

1085
01:03:48,234 --> 01:03:56,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would urge and cause... evoke sense of urgency in sotāpānas, sakadāgāmīs and anāgāmīs, because they, as he said, they still have work to do.

1086
01:03:58,033 --> 01:04:08,203
[SPEAKER_01]: But, knowing the way out of that crumbling house will free you from that fundamental existential fear of not knowing the way out.

1087
01:04:08,223 --> 01:04:09,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because I can just step out.

1088
01:04:09,264 --> 01:04:10,024
[SPEAKER_01]: I can just step out.

1089
01:04:10,044 --> 01:04:10,905
[SPEAKER_01]: There's my door, I'm right there.

1090
01:04:10,925 --> 01:04:17,031
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why many sotāpannas would, like, become sotāpannas or even, like, many ārya-sāvakus become ārahant at the moment of death.

1091
01:04:17,051 --> 01:04:19,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the moment of death is the moment of being thrown out.

1092
01:04:19,673 --> 01:04:20,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because you know the way out.

1093
01:04:20,734 --> 01:04:22,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know the way out and that's what you understood.

1094
01:04:22,957 --> 01:04:23,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1095
01:04:23,818 --> 01:04:33,026
[SPEAKER_00]: But for the person with the bright view, he knows which direction is nibbāna, which way leads to nibbāna.

1096
01:04:33,046 --> 01:04:34,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he knows where nibbāna is.

1097
01:04:34,967 --> 01:04:39,251
[SPEAKER_01]: He now just needs to adhere to it sufficiently long enough.

1098
01:04:39,451 --> 01:04:41,952
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't have to now start again each time.

1099
01:04:42,112 --> 01:04:42,693
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1100
01:04:43,473 --> 01:04:44,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Is sensuality bad?

1101
01:04:44,634 --> 01:04:46,995
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course, because he's beyond... he's freed himself from doubt.

1102
01:04:47,255 --> 01:04:48,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It's so clear.

1103
01:04:48,116 --> 01:04:48,856
[SPEAKER_01]: He cannot doubt it.

1104
01:04:49,116 --> 01:04:50,437
[SPEAKER_01]: It's so clear he cannot doubt it.

1105
01:04:50,597 --> 01:04:57,041
[SPEAKER_00]: It's so... that is so clear that he cannot doubt it, but he knows what to do then and there.

1106
01:04:57,141 --> 01:04:58,902
[SPEAKER_01]: And he knows what to do because he cannot doubt it.

1107
01:04:59,102 --> 01:04:59,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1108
01:05:01,382 --> 01:05:04,224
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he doesn't need to... oh, so is this bad?

1109
01:05:04,444 --> 01:05:09,788
[SPEAKER_01]: He understood it's bad and even the question is this bad is meaningless because the understanding that it's bad is clear.

1110
01:05:09,848 --> 01:05:11,710
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely familiar.

1111
01:05:16,613 --> 01:05:18,615
[SPEAKER_01]: And not like familiar with all this information.

1112
01:05:19,595 --> 01:05:19,735
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1113
01:05:19,835 --> 01:05:21,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply familiar with where the problem is.

1114
01:05:23,478 --> 01:05:25,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing problem as a problem and non-problem as non-problem.

1115
01:05:27,342 --> 01:05:32,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it a quick wisdom, that kind of lightning wisdom?

1116
01:05:32,446 --> 01:05:34,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It's with seeing things as they are, yeah.

1117
01:05:34,508 --> 01:05:35,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But that takes time to develop.

1118
01:05:36,509 --> 01:05:38,951
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not have the quick wisdom, necessarily, that quick.

1119
01:05:39,472 --> 01:05:42,614
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's quick, sufficiently enough, so to speak.

1120
01:05:43,114 --> 01:05:46,757
[SPEAKER_01]: But not quick wisdom in a sense of it quickly responds to there is a problem.

1121
01:05:47,858 --> 01:05:50,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Wisdom basically means when you see beyond there is a problem.

1122
01:05:51,060 --> 01:05:53,082
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot not see the nature of the problem.

1123
01:05:54,447 --> 01:05:56,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's... that's like... it's so clear.

1124
01:05:56,168 --> 01:05:58,209
[SPEAKER_01]: The first noble truth is the truth of suffering.

1125
01:05:58,850 --> 01:05:59,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the problem is.

1126
01:06:01,131 --> 01:06:05,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot mistake non-problem as problem.

1127
01:06:07,434 --> 01:06:10,316
[SPEAKER_01]: By knowing what the problem is, you then know how that problem is there.

1128
01:06:11,277 --> 01:06:13,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Why that... why that is a problem.

1129
01:06:13,678 --> 01:06:16,740
[SPEAKER_01]: By knowing why that is a problem, you know then what it isn't a problem.

1130
01:06:17,180 --> 01:06:18,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you will know the way out of the problem.

1131
01:06:19,341 --> 01:06:21,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the four noble truths are basically one inside.

1132
01:06:23,202 --> 01:06:24,283
[SPEAKER_01]: In these different directions.

1133
01:06:25,343 --> 01:06:27,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding suffering means understanding the way out of it.

1134
01:06:28,825 --> 01:06:31,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Because suffering is not about figuring out information.

1135
01:06:31,967 --> 01:06:33,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the kind of things that make you suffer.

1136
01:06:34,048 --> 01:06:44,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is about not resisting pain, not leaning towards pleasure, not leaning away from pain, and not kind of turning a blind eye to neither pleasure nor pain.

1137
01:06:45,495 --> 01:06:48,177
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the root of suffering and that's what he has understood.

1138
01:06:49,082 --> 01:06:52,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, no amount of things that come from the world can be mistaken as suffering.

1139
01:06:53,064 --> 01:06:55,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Only craving arises, only craving ceases.

1140
01:06:55,765 --> 01:07:05,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Or as those in the Terra Terrigata, the verses, when, like, Mara comes and tries to, sort of, disturb their mind.

1141
01:07:05,089 --> 01:07:07,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, is this, or what if they, what if the robbers come?

1142
01:07:07,370 --> 01:07:09,011
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, what are we talking about?

1143
01:07:09,031 --> 01:07:10,332
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing for me to protect.

1144
01:07:11,172 --> 01:07:17,535
[SPEAKER_01]: None of the things that are being threatened, even by possible robber or rapist or whatever else, none of those things are suffering.

1145
01:07:19,048 --> 01:07:20,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is the suffering, basically.

1146
01:07:20,508 --> 01:07:25,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering within this arises, suffering ceases on its own, because craving arises, craving ceases on its own.

1147
01:07:25,710 --> 01:07:28,870
[SPEAKER_01]: All I have to do is not act out of it, because now I understood it.

1148
01:07:30,131 --> 01:07:31,251
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no other problem.

1149
01:07:32,831 --> 01:07:37,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Who are you talking about when you say, oh, you should do this, or defend that, or run away from this forest?

1150
01:07:37,152 --> 01:07:38,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Who are you talking about?

1151
01:07:40,393 --> 01:07:42,994
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the only reason you would run out of the way of the forest is because it makes you suffer.

1152
01:07:43,474 --> 01:07:47,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you don't see that suffering that you feel is the problem, you think the forest is the problem.

1153
01:07:48,878 --> 01:07:50,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, only suffering arises, suffering ceases.

1154
01:07:50,419 --> 01:07:51,119
[SPEAKER_01]: What else is there?

1155
01:07:51,219 --> 01:07:51,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

1156
01:07:52,160 --> 01:07:53,060
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

1157
01:07:53,180 --> 01:08:00,004
[SPEAKER_01]: All I teach, I mean, as many suttas as there are, all I teach is suffering and freedom from it.

1158
01:08:00,584 --> 01:08:02,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else he ever said is within that.

1159
01:08:03,165 --> 01:08:03,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1160
01:08:04,626 --> 01:08:05,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering and freedom from it.

1161
01:08:05,627 --> 01:08:08,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this all simplifies to... the clarity is...

1162
01:08:08,168 --> 01:08:11,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to me, the clarity of Sotāpanna is not the clarity... the clarity of Sotāpanna is, ah,

1163
01:08:12,660 --> 01:08:13,180
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what matters.

1164
01:08:13,200 --> 01:08:14,241
[SPEAKER_01]: There is suffering.

1165
01:08:14,261 --> 01:08:15,802
[SPEAKER_01]: There is freedom from suffering.

1166
01:08:15,842 --> 01:08:16,443
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what matters.

1167
01:08:16,543 --> 01:08:17,503
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only thing that matters.

1168
01:08:18,024 --> 01:08:20,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Resisting a feeling or not doing so.

1169
01:08:20,345 --> 01:08:21,626
[SPEAKER_01]: That's only the only thing that matters.

1170
01:08:24,088 --> 01:08:27,190
